DOGE-15 — Trump-Branded Tokens, Meme-Coin Purchases, Anonymous Foreign Buyers, and the Alleged Sale of Presidential Access

I. Investigative purpose

This exhibit examines whether purchases and continued holdings of the $TRUMP meme coin were used as a mechanism for domestic or foreign persons to obtain access to President Donald J. Trump while providing economic benefits to Trump-associated entities.

The investigation focuses on:

the ownership and control of the token supply;
revenue from initial sales, liquidity pools, and secondary trading;
the offer of a presidential dinner and VIP reception to leading token holders;
the identity and nationality of qualifying wallet owners;
possible relationships between purchasers and pending government matters;
use of presidential status, travel, security, property, or communications;
the SEC’s contemporaneous position that typical meme coins are not securities;
possible campaign-finance, bribery, emoluments, fraud, or foreign-influence violations; and
whether the arrangement formed part of a broader enterprise monetizing governmental authority.

The dinner and purchase competition present an unusually direct connection between token ownership and presidential access. They do not, without proof of a corrupt agreement or legally prohibited benefit, conclusively establish bribery.

II. Principal persons and entities

Potential subjects, witnesses, and record custodians include:

Donald J. Trump;
Trump-family business representatives;
the Donald J. Trump Revocable Trust;
CIC Digital LLC;
Fight Fight Fight LLC;
Celebration Cards LLC;
the Trump Organization;
the $TRUMP token’s developers and administrators;
token treasury and liquidity-pool controllers;
market makers;
Solana validators and analytics providers;
centralized exchanges listing $TRUMP;
the top 220 dinner qualifiers;
the top 25 VIP qualifiers;
Justin Sun and relevant affiliated entities;
unidentified foreign wallet owners;
participating corporations and executives;
White House personnel;
Secret Service personnel;
Trump National Golf Club Washington, D.C.;
SEC, DOJ, FEC, OGE, FinCEN, and OFAC personnel;
DOGE personnel possessing relevant access or communications; and
intermediaries who solicited purchases, arranged access, or communicated official requests.

Identification does not imply wrongdoing.

III. Creation of the $TRUMP token

The $TRUMP token was launched shortly before President Trump’s January 20, 2025 inauguration and was promoted through Trump-associated communications.

The token was marketed as:

an expression of support;
a digital collectible;
entertainment rather than an investment;
unrelated to a political campaign or government office; and
not an investment contract or security.

Those labels are relevant but not dispositive. Legal classification depends upon the transaction’s economic reality, representations, allocation, promoter conduct, and purchaser expectations.

IV. Ownership concentration

Public materials stated that CIC Digital LLC and Fight Fight Fight LLC collectively controlled approximately 80 percent of the one-billion-token supply, subject to an unlocking schedule.

Only approximately 200 million tokens were initially released for public circulation, while approximately 800 million remained associated with the issuer-controlled allocation.

This concentration created potential control over:

circulating supply;
unlock timing;
liquidity;
market depth;
price volatility;
sales into public demand;
exchange listings;
promotional incentives; and
revenue derived from trading activity.

Concentration is not inherently unlawful. It becomes material if insiders misrepresented supply, manipulated trading, concealed sales, or used official announcements to create exit liquidity.

V. Revenue channels

Trump-associated entities could potentially receive economic benefits through several channels:

initial token sales;
liquidity-pool transactions;
trading fees;
licensing payments;
market-making arrangements;
appreciation of retained tokens;
collateralization or lending;
exchange-listing agreements;
promotional partnerships; and
future token unlocks.

The notional market value of retained tokens is not equivalent to realized income.

The investigation must calculate separately:

cash and stablecoins actually received;
trading fees;
withdrawn liquidity;
unrealized token value;
related-party transfers;
market-maker compensation; and
amounts ultimately distributed to beneficial owners.

VI. Announcement of the presidential-access competition

On April 23, 2025, the token’s promotional website announced that leading $TRUMP holders would be eligible for an event with President Trump.

The advertised structure reportedly included:

dinner access for the top 220 holders;
a VIP reception for the top 25 holders;
an opportunity to hear from the President;
additional benefits for the highest-ranking purchasers; and
eligibility based on token holdings measured during a specified period.

The dinner was scheduled for May 22, 2025, at Trump National Golf Club Washington, D.C.

Senators Adam Schiff and Elizabeth Warren requested an OGE inquiry, describing the arrangement as posing a serious “pay-to-play” risk. Their letter is an allegation and oversight request, not a judicial finding. Senate letter to OGE, April 25, 2025

VII. Direct connection between payment and access

The promotion differed from ordinary political fundraising because eligibility was determined through ownership of a privately issued asset rather than a reported political contribution.

The operative sequence was:

flowchart TD
    A["Buyer acquires or retains $TRUMP"] --> B["Wallet rises on leaderboard"]
    B --> C["Top 220 qualify for dinner"]
    C --> D["Top 25 qualify for VIP reception"]
    A --> E["Trading volume and fees increase"]
    E --> F["Trump-associated entities may benefit"]
This establishes a direct promotional connection between purchasing or retaining the token and eligibility for presidential access.
The unresolved issue is whether the access was purely a commercial promotional benefit or an opportunity to influence governmental decisions.
VIII. Purchase versus contribution
A $TRUMP purchase was not formally processed as a campaign contribution.
That distinction carries significant consequences:
Campaign contributionToken purchaseDonor identity may have to be disclosedWallet may remain pseudonymousContribution limits may applyNo corresponding purchase limitForeign-national prohibition expressly appliesApplication depends on purpose and structureFunds go to political committeeValue may benefit private entitiesFEC reporting and disclaimer rules applyMay occur through exchanges or decentralized marketsCoordination rules applyCommercial transaction may obscure political purpose
The token structure could therefore permit benefits exceeding campaign-contribution limits while avoiding ordinary donor disclosure.

That does not automatically make the purchase an unlawful contribution. Investigators must establish whether the payment was made for the purpose of influencing an election or obtaining official action.

IX. Leaderboard methodology

Investigators should reconstruct exactly how dinner eligibility was calculated:

whether ranking depended on total purchases or average holdings;

whether tokens had to remain in a registered wallet;
whether exchange-held tokens qualified;
whether multiple wallets could be combined;
whether transfers between related wallets were counted;
whether insiders or market makers appeared on the leaderboard;
whether borrowed or temporarily transferred tokens qualified;
whether wash trades increased a participant’s ranking;
whether organizers altered rankings manually; and
whether exceptions were granted.
The raw leaderboard is not proof that the displayed name was the wallet’s beneficial owner.
X. Anonymous and pseudonymous purchasers
Public blockchain addresses reveal transactions but ordinarily do not reveal verified identity or nationality.
A leaderboard pseudonym could represent:
an individual;
a corporation;
an exchange omnibus account;
a fund;

a nominee;

multiple beneficial owners;

a foreign government or sovereign entity;

a sanctioned person;

a market maker;
the issuer itself; or
a wallet controlled by an intermediary.
Accordingly, claims that a particular number of attendees were foreign must be treated as analytical estimates unless supported by know-your-customer, travel, invitation, or identity records.
XI. Congressional foreign-buyer allegations
A group of House members requested a DOJ Public Integrity Section investigation on May 22, 2025.
Their letter alleged that:
many leading wallets appeared likely to be foreign-controlled;
some received funds from exchanges unavailable to U.S. customers;
Justin Sun occupied a leading position;

purchasers spent approximately $145–148 million during the competition period;

some businesses publicly connected purchases with desired government policy; and

numerous winners reduced their holdings after qualification.

These are substantial investigative leads, but the underlying blockchain attribution and commercial data must be independently verified. House request for DOJ investigation

XII. The May 22 dinner
President Trump attended the May 22, 2025 dinner at his Virginia-area golf property.
The investigation should establish:
the complete guest list;
the actual beneficial owner behind each qualifying wallet;
seating arrangements;
VIP reception attendees;
duration of presidential participation;
remarks delivered;
private conversations;
photographs and recordings;

materials distributed;

follow-up communications;

White House or Secret Service involvement; and

whether governmental matters were discussed.

Attendance proves access. It does not establish that the attendee requested or received governmental action.
XIII. Presidential versus private capacity
A central question is whether President Trump attended:
as President;
as a private licensor or businessman;
as a political figure;

or in multiple capacities simultaneously.

Relevant factors include:

how he was introduced;

whether presidential insignia were used;

whether White House staff organized the event;
whether official remarks were delivered;
whether the event appeared on an official schedule;
whether government communications promoted it;
whether Secret Service or military transportation was used;
whether costs were reimbursed; and
whether attendees reasonably understood they were purchasing access to the President.
A disclaimer stating that the token was unrelated to government does not necessarily resolve contrary real-world conduct.
XIV. Use of government resources
Investigators should calculate whether taxpayers paid for:
presidential air or ground transportation;
Marine One operations;

Secret Service travel and protective staffing;

White House advance personnel;

communications support;

security sweeps;
classified communications capability;
local law-enforcement support; and
overtime or logistical expenses.

Protective services ordinarily follow the President even during private or political travel. The relevant issue is whether legally required reimbursement was made and whether public resources materially supported a private revenue-generating event.

XV. Trump-property revenue
Because the dinner occurred at a Trump-branded property, investigators should obtain:
venue agreements;
event invoices;
food and beverage charges;
security charges;
rental fees;
staffing costs;
payments from the token entities;

payments to Trump-affiliated businesses;

discounts or waived fees;

reimbursement records; and

resulting profit.
This represents a potential second revenue stream distinct from token trading.
XVI. Promotional price effect
The dinner announcement reportedly produced a sharp increase in $TRUMP demand, trading volume, and price.
That market reaction was foreseeable because obtaining access required participants to acquire or retain the token.
Investigators should determine whether insiders:
purchased before the announcement;
sold after the price increase;
delayed scheduled unlocks;

removed liquidity;

transferred tokens to market makers;

used knowledge of the promotion before publication;

coordinated with exchanges;
opened short or derivative positions;
used related wallets to stimulate trading; or
concealed beneficial ownership of trades.
A price increase caused by disclosed promotional activity is not automatically manipulation. Deception, artificial trading, undisclosed insider conduct, or a manipulative scheme would require separate proof.
XVII. SEC’s meme-coin statement
On February 27, 2025, the SEC Division of Corporation Finance issued a staff statement expressing the view that transactions involving the typical meme coins described in the statement generally do not constitute securities transactions.
The statement reasoned that typical meme coins:
have limited or no utility;
are purchased for entertainment, cultural, or social purposes;
do not provide rights to business income or assets; and

depend on speculative market sentiment rather than managerial efforts.

The staff also emphasized that:

the statement was not a Commission rule;

it had no legal force;

it did not cover products labeled meme coins to evade securities law;

classification depends on the specific economic reality; and
fraud may remain prosecutable under other federal or state laws.
SEC Division of Corporation Finance, meme-coin statement
XVIII. Timing of the SEC statement
The statement was issued:
after the $TRUMP token’s January launch;
while the President retained financial connections to cryptocurrency ventures;
before the April dinner announcement; and
during a broader administration shift away from digital-asset enforcement.
Investigators should determine:

whether SEC staff analyzed $TRUMP specifically;

whether White House or industry officials requested the statement;

whether the token’s access promotion would change the analysis;

whether purchasers reasonably expected profit from Trump’s official visibility;

whether issuer-controlled supply constituted a common enterprise;
whether promoter activity was economically significant; and
whether career staff objected.
The statement itself says that a definitive classification requires analysis of the particular transaction.

XIX. Access as token utility

The dinner promotion complicates the claim that $TRUMP had no utility.
During the competition, holding the token could provide:
eligibility for an exclusive event;
enhanced access based on ranking;
a VIP reception;

possible merchandise or collectible benefits; and

proximity to the President.

That does not necessarily transform the token into a security. It does show that the token temporarily had a concrete access-related function beyond abstract entertainment or political expression.

XX. Expectation-of-profit inquiry
Investigators should preserve evidence concerning whether purchasers expected profits from:
presidential promotion;
favorable cryptocurrency policy;

exchange listings;

the dinner announcement;
issuer-controlled scarcity;
token unlock management;
White House events;
the Strategic Bitcoin Reserve;
regulatory relief; or
continued use of the Trump name and presidency.

A purchaser’s subjective hope of price appreciation is not enough by itself. The inquiry must examine promises, marketing, promoter conduct, and economic structure.

XXI. Justin Sun nexus

Public and congressional materials identified Justin Sun as a leading $TRUMP holder and dinner participant or qualifier.

Sun had also reportedly:

purchased substantial World Liberty Financial tokens;
served as an adviser to World Liberty Financial;
participated in Tron-related integration;
held interests affected by U.S. digital-asset policy; and
faced an SEC civil action that was later stayed.

This combination requires examination of:

Sun’s qualifying wallets;

the sources of purchase funds;

his dinner and VIP access;
discussions with Trump or administration officials;
communications with World Liberty Financial;
SEC contacts;
requests for policy or enforcement relief; and
transactions occurring before and after government action.
The sequence does not prove that Sun purchased regulatory relief.
XXII. Corporate purchasers seeking policy influence
Congressional investigators identified public statements from companies that connected contemplated $TRUMP purchases with interests in government policy.
One reported example involved a transportation company describing a token-purchase strategy alongside advocacy for favorable U.S.–Mexico trade relations.

Another involved a company with China-related operations proposing a substantial acquisition of $TRUMP.

These public announcements require verification through:

SEC filings;

board minutes;

financing records;
wallet transactions;
beneficial ownership;
actual purchase completion;
lobbying communications; and

subsequent government action.

An announced plan is not proof that the tokens were purchased.
XXIII. Foreign-national campaign-finance theory
Federal law generally prohibits foreign nationals from directly or indirectly contributing or donating money or another thing of value in connection with American elections.
A foreign person’s commercial token purchase is not automatically covered.
Potential campaign-finance liability would require evidence that:
the purchase was intended to influence an election;
it was coordinated with a campaign or political committee;
the dinner constituted a campaign event;

the token price substantially exceeded genuine commercial value;

the transaction was a disguised donation;

campaign expenses were paid from token proceeds; or

an American intermediary knowingly facilitated a prohibited foreign contribution.

Because the event occurred after the election, bribery, emoluments, ethics, and official-access theories may be more directly relevant than election-finance law unless continuing campaign activity is established.

XXIV. Bribery theory

A bribery theory would require proof that a purchaser provided or promised something of value corruptly in exchange for an official act.
The alleged value could include:
token purchases;
trading fees;
price support;
increased liquidity;
venue payments;
commercial partnerships;

investment in another Trump-associated venture; or

benefits to family members or affiliated entities.

The alleged official act would have to be identified with specificity, such as:

intervention in a named enforcement matter;

an executive order;

clemency;
an export license;
sanctions relief;
a regulatory appointment;
a contract;
a tariff or trade decision; or
agency action concerning the purchaser.

Dinner access alone is not necessarily an official act. It may provide the opportunity to request or influence one.

XXV. Gratuity and access-purchase concerns

Even when no prior agreement can be proven, investigators should examine whether value was supplied because of an official act already performed or expected.

Federal gratuity law has been narrowed by Supreme Court decisions and cannot be treated as a general prohibition on every gift connected with official status.

The investigation must identify:
the covered public official;
the particular official act;
the timing of payment;
the statutory theory;
the donor’s intent; and
whether an applicable exception or limitation applies.
XXVI. Foreign Emoluments Clause

Purchases by foreign governments or their instrumentalities may raise questions under the Foreign Emoluments Clause if they confer profit, gain, or advantage on the President without congressional consent.

Required proof includes:
purchaser identity;
foreign-state control;
economic benefit received by the President;
ownership and control of the receiving entities;
whether the transaction was bona fide and at market value;
whether token price included a premium for presidential access; and
whether Congress consented.
The application of the Clause to indirect business transactions remains contested and should not be stated as conclusively resolved.

XXVII. Domestic Emoluments Clause

The Domestic Emoluments Clause prohibits the President from receiving additional emoluments from the United States or a state during the elected term beyond presidential compensation.

Possible issues could arise if:

government agencies purchased tokens;

public funds paid private event expenses without reimbursement;

state entities provided special benefits;
public pension funds acquired the token for political reasons; or
governmental action directly transferred value to the President’s associated entities.
Ordinary Secret Service protection does not itself establish an additional prohibited emolument.
XXVIII. Fraud and disclosure theory
A possible fraud theory would require material deception concerning:

issuer ownership;

insider allocations;

token unlocks;

liquidity;
market-making activity;
trading revenue;
access benefits;
guest selection;
foreign participation;
investment risk;

political or governmental affiliation;

security-law status; or

the identity of persons receiving proceeds.

Disclaimers are relevant but do not immunize affirmative misrepresentations or deceptive conduct.

XXIX. Market-manipulation theory
Potential indicators include:
wash trading;
self-dealing among controlled wallets;
spoofed liquidity;

coordinated purchases;

undisclosed market-maker arrangements;

misleading supply information;

artificial leaderboard activity;
secret insider sales;
delayed unlocks designed to influence price;
coordinated exchange announcements; and
trading based on nonpublic presidential-event information.
Volatility and speculation alone do not prove manipulation.
XXX. Consumer-protection gap
If the token was not a security, purchasers may have lacked protections ordinarily available under federal securities law, including:
registration disclosures;
audited issuer financial statements;
insider ownership reporting;
restrictions on manipulative trading under securities statutes;
regulated-exchange requirements;

broker-dealer duties; and

established private remedies.

Other authorities may still apply, including:

state fraud laws;
consumer-protection statutes;
commodities anti-fraud and anti-manipulation provisions;
wire-fraud law;
money-transmission law;
sanctions rules; and
general criminal law.
XXXI. Wallet-attribution protocol
Each leading wallet should be investigated through:
complete blockchain transaction history;
funding-source analysis;

exchange deposit and withdrawal records;

lawful acquisition of know-your-customer records;

bank transfers;

custody agreements;
device and login evidence;
corporate beneficial-ownership records;
invitation and identity-verification records;
passport and travel information;
sanctions screening;
communications with organizers;

linked World Liberty or USD1 transactions; and

post-dinner trading.
Blockchain clustering should be corroborated and not treated as conclusive identity evidence.
XXXII. Access-to-action tracing
For every attendee, investigators should build a before-and-after chronology:
government matter pending before the event;
token-acquisition date;
leaderboard position;

communications requesting access;

dinner or VIP participation;

subjects discussed;
follow-up contacts;
agency referrals;
official decisions;
token disposals; and
additional payments or investments.
This permits the investigation to distinguish speculative participation from a possible exchange.
XXXIII. DOGE connection
The current public record does not establish that DOGE organized the token, dinner, guest list, or transactions.
A DOGE connection would require evidence that DOGE personnel:
attended or facilitated the event;
supplied government information to purchasers;
communicated with qualifying wallets or their representatives;
influenced matters affecting attendees;

intervened in regulatory investigations;

accessed SEC, Treasury, FinCEN, or other protected records;

used DOGE authority to recommend personnel or enforcement changes; or

received token, investment, employment, or political benefits.
Until that evidence exists, DOGE should be described as a possible governmental coordination node rather than a proven participant.
XXXIV. RICO analysis
For DOGE-15 to support a RICO theory, investigators must prove more than commercialization of presidential access.
Required elements would include:
an identifiable enterprise;
knowing participation by each defendant;
qualifying predicate acts such as bribery, wire fraud, money laundering, obstruction, or another enumerated offense;
a relationship between those predicates;
continuity or threat of repetition;
use of interstate or foreign commerce;

proceeds or benefits flowing through the enterprise; and

injury proximately caused by the racketeering conduct.

The dinner itself is not automatically a RICO predicate.

DOGE-51 — EXHIBIT D.O.G.E.-PR-1: UNIKORNAIO Press Release — “Giving Up on DC” Remarks, Alleged D.O.G.E. National Security Risk, and Obstruction-by-Appointment Pattern

Exhibit designation: D.O.G.E.-PR-1
Date of statement: December 24, 2025
Location: Oklahoma City, Oklahoma
Source: UNIKORNAIO Press Release / Public Investigative Notice
Evidentiary classification: Party or declarant statement offered to establish publication, notice, chronology, and UNIKORNAIO’s contemporaneous investigative position—not, standing alone, the truth of every allegation contained in the statement.
I. Purpose of exhibit
This exhibit preserves UNIKORNAIO’s December 24, 2025 public statement concerning:
public remarks attributed to Elon Musk about his experience attempting to reform the federal government;
the retreat from DOGE’s original public promises;

alleged placement of insufficiently screened, conflicted, inexperienced, or inadequately supervised personnel in sensitive federal environments;

weakening of institutional safeguards;

potential exposure of government systems and information;

alleged interference with investigators and oversight bodies;
and the working theory that government appointments, access grants, personnel reductions, and reorganizations may have functioned as indirect methods of obstruction.
The exhibit establishes that UNIKORNAIO placed these concerns on the public record by December 24, 2025.
The press release does not independently establish:
the precise wording of Musk’s remarks;
that every DOGE employee was unqualified;
that a particular appointment was made with obstructive intent;

that DOGE personnel altered sensitive systems;

that Trump or Musk intended to obstruct a specific proceeding;

or that DOGE constituted a criminal enterprise.
Those propositions require separate corroborating evidence.
II. Revised legal framework
Potentially relevant authorities depend on the evidence ultimately obtained.
A. RICO
The Racketeer Influenced and Corrupt Organizations Act, 18 U.S.C. §§ 1961–1968, may become relevant only if evidence establishes:

an enterprise;

participation in the enterprise’s affairs;

a pattern of qualifying predicate offenses;

and, for civil RICO, a concrete business-or-property injury proximately caused by the predicates.

Institutional dysfunction, negligent appointments, conflicts of interest, or administrative illegality are not independently RICO predicates.

B. Obstruction statutes

Potentially relevant obstruction provisions include:
18 U.S.C. § 1503, where conduct corruptly affects a pending federal judicial proceeding and the required nexus is established;
18 U.S.C. § 1510, where bribery is used to obstruct communication of information concerning a federal criminal violation;
18 U.S.C. § 1512, where conduct involves witnesses, evidence, official proceedings, intimidation, corrupt persuasion, or qualifying obstruction;

18 U.S.C. § 1519, where records are knowingly altered, destroyed, concealed, or falsified with intent to impede or influence a federal matter or bankruptcy; and

other matter-specific obstruction provisions where their elements are satisfied.

The phrase “obstruction by appointment” is an investigative description, not a separately codified federal offense. Liability requires proof that staffing or appointment authority was used with the intent and legally required nexus to obstruct a particular investigation, proceeding, communication, record, or federal matter.

C. False statements and concealment
Under 18 U.S.C. § 1001, liability may arise from knowingly and willfully:
falsifying or concealing a material fact through a trick, scheme, or device;
making a materially false statement;
or using a materially false document

within federal jurisdiction, subject to statutory limitations and exceptions.

Political rhetoric or a disputed public characterization is not automatically a § 1001 offense.

D. Conspiracy against the United States

Under 18 U.S.C. § 371, the government generally must prove:
an agreement between at least two persons;
to commit an offense against the United States or defraud the United States;

knowing and voluntary participation;

and an overt act.

Parallel conduct or shared political objectives do not alone establish an agreement.

E. Honest-services fraud and public integrity

Mail and wire fraud under 18 U.S.C. §§ 1341 and 1343 may incorporate honest-services fraud under § 1346 only within the doctrine’s controlling limits, principally bribery and kickback schemes.
Undisclosed self-interest or poor governance does not automatically constitute honest-services fraud.
F. Ethics and conflicts
Potentially relevant authorities include:

18 U.S.C. § 208;

federal ethics regulations;

financial-disclosure requirements;

procurement-integrity rules;

organizational-conflict rules;

recusals and waivers;

and post-government employment restrictions.
G. Security and internal controls
Relevant standards may include:
FISMA;
NIST security controls;
agency authorities to operate;
suitability and background-screening requirements;

classified-information and controlled-unclassified-information rules;

least-privilege requirements;

incident reporting;
and agency-specific access controls.
A violation of a security standard is not necessarily a criminal offense. It may establish negligence, policy noncompliance, administrative liability, or evidence supporting another claim.
H. International public-integrity authorities
The United Nations Convention Against Corruption and related international standards may provide context concerning:
public-sector integrity;
conflicts;
procurement;

bribery;

misuse of public functions;

and protection of reporting persons.

They do not automatically create a private federal cause of action.
III. Statement of facts contained in the press release
On December 24, 2025, UNIKORNAIO issued a public investigative statement responding to circulated remarks attributed to Elon Musk concerning his experience with DOGE and attempting to reform Washington.
Contemporaneous reporting substantiates that Musk publicly expressed substantial disappointment with DOGE. In a December 2025 interview, he described DOGE as only “somewhat successful” and indicated that, knowing what he knew afterward, he would not undertake the effort again. Associated Press report on Musk’s December 2025 DOGE remarks
However, the precise quotation that Musk had “given up on trying to fix Washington, D.C.” should remain characterized as attributed or paraphrased unless the original audio, video, transcript, or social-media post confirms those exact words.
UNIKORNAIO interpreted the remarks as part of a possible narrative sequence in which:

an actor publicly promises transformative reform;

associates receive sensitive appointments and government access;

institutional safeguards and experienced personnel are reduced;

disruptions are characterized as evidence of preexisting government incompetence;

accountability for the disruptions is redirected toward the institution itself; and

the actor later withdraws while claiming that reform was impossible.

This interpretation is UNIKORNAIO’s stated investigative theory. It is not an adjudicated finding of intent.

IV. Publicly established DOGE retreat

The public record supports several narrower propositions:

Musk assumed a highly visible role associated with DOGE;

DOGE promoted extensive personnel, contract, grant, program, and systems changes;

Musk later stepped back from government service;

Musk subsequently described the project as only partly successful;

he indicated that he would probably not repeat the experience;

and he attributed substantial difficulty to entrenched government systems, political opposition, and negative consequences for his businesses.

These facts establish retreat from the original transformational posture. They do not, by themselves, show that the retreat concealed completed obstruction or sabotage.

V. UNIKORNAIO’s institutional-degradation theory

UNIKORNAIO’s press release identified a recurring operational pattern:
entry into an institution under the banner of reform;
replacement or marginalization of experienced personnel;
installation of politically or personally trusted personnel;
loosening of access and reporting controls;
concentration of decision-making;

reduction of independent oversight;

creation of operational disorder;

attribution of that disorder to the institution;

and withdrawal after the actor’s private or defensive objectives have allegedly been achieved.

Under this theory, dysfunction may serve three purposes:
Camouflage: It makes intentional interference difficult to distinguish from ordinary incompetence.
Causation laundering: Damage produced by the reform effort is attributed to the preexisting institution.
Investigative disruption: Auditors and investigators lose personnel, records, access, jurisdiction, or organizational continuity.
Each asserted purpose requires evidence of intent. Disorder alone is insufficient.
VI. Alleged security-dilution strategy
UNIKORNAIO described a possible security-dilution strategy in which risk is created by distributing sensitive access among inadequately screened, poorly supervised, conflicted, temporary, or inexperienced personnel.
Possible indicators include:
access granted before security training;

privileges broader than approved;

lack of signed rules of behavior;

insufficient background screening;

unclear supervisory authority;

use of personal or external systems;

inadequate logging;

improper cross-agency transmission;

missing exit procedures;

and continued private employment involving affected industries.

GAO later documented several relevant control deficiencies at Treasury. It found that a DOGE-team employee received a government laptop before Treasury ensured agreement to security rules, possessed broad access to payment information, temporarily received unintended modification privileges, and improperly transmitted unencrypted payment information to another agency DOGE team. GAO found no evidence that the temporary privileges were used to alter system data. GAO, Treasury Needs to Fully Implement Data Protection Controls
These findings corroborate the existence of control weaknesses. They do not establish deliberate security dilution.
VII. Qualification must be measured against assigned function
The label “unqualified” should not rest on age, political association, private-sector background, or lack of conventional government service alone.
Investigators should evaluate whether each appointee or employee possessed the qualifications required for the actual assignment, including:
relevant technical competence;
security and privacy training;
suitability determination;
required clearance;
statutory appointment authority;

understanding of the agency’s mission;

procurement or financial-system expertise;

conflict disclosure;

and appropriate supervision.

A technically capable engineer may still be unqualified to make legal, personnel, benefits, or enforcement decisions. Conversely, lack of prior federal service does not prove technical incompetence.

VIII. Personal loyalty versus institutional duty

The press release alleged that personal loyalty may have displaced ordinary clearance discipline and risk management.
Relevant evidence would include:
recruitment based on personal affiliation;
instructions to report outside the ordinary chain of command;
pressure to disregard agency counsel;
avoidance of ethics or security review;
secret reporting channels;
refusal to identify personnel;
use of unpaid or dual-employed workers;

and adverse action against officials who denied access.

The issue is not whether political leadership may appoint trusted personnel. It is whether loyalty was used to defeat statutory duties, security controls, independent judgment, or lawful oversight.

IX. Obstruction-by-appointment theory

UNIKORNAIO uses the phrase obstruction by appointment to describe an alleged mechanism in which staffing authority is used to impede oversight indirectly.

Potential forms include:

appointing a conflicted official to supervise an investigation involving an affiliate;
replacing an inspector or investigator with someone directed to close the matter;
installing personnel who deny necessary resources;
granting sensitive access to the subject of an investigation or its associates;
assigning unqualified personnel in a manner calculated to destroy continuity;
restructuring reporting lines to prevent escalation;
and using appointments to obtain confidential case information.
An appointment becomes potentially obstructive only when supported by evidence concerning:

a foreseeable or pending federal matter;

the appointer’s knowledge;

the appointee’s assigned authority;

conduct affecting the matter;

corrupt intent where required;

and a sufficient relationship between the appointment and the obstruction.
X. Appointment authority is not an immunity
The President and agency heads possess substantial appointment and supervisory authority. That authority does not necessarily immunize independently criminal conduct.
At the same time, courts will not infer obstruction merely because:
an administration replaced policy officials;
an appointee disagreed with career staff;
enforcement priorities changed;

or an agency was reorganized.

The investigation must identify the particular protected proceeding, investigation, witness, record, or governmental function allegedly targeted.
XI. Neutralization of investigators
The press release asserted that federal-adjacent entry may have served to neutralize persons investigating affiliated actors.
Potential neutralization methods include:
termination;
reassignment;

revocation of system access;

cancellation of contracts supporting investigations;

elimination of offices;

reduction of budgets;

exposure of whistleblower identities;
access to investigative files;
disruption of evidence preservation;
public attacks on investigators;

and expiration of statutory deadlines.

A credible analysis must identify:

the investigator;

the underlying matter;

the target;

the personnel action;
the responsible decision-maker;
and the effect on the investigation.
General reductions affecting an agency do not establish intentional neutralization of a particular investigator.
XII. Protection of Trump and affiliated actors
UNIKORNAIO’s press release alleged that DOGE’s reform narrative may have functioned as a protective shield for President Trump, Musk, and associated interests.
That allegation must be separated into testable questions:
Did DOGE personnel gain access to investigations involving Trump, Musk, or affiliated companies?
Were responsible investigators removed or reassigned?
Were enforcement matters closed or delayed?
Were records altered, exported, or concealed?

Did DOGE personnel communicate with investigative targets?

Did staffing or budget changes selectively affect those matters?
Were similar matters involving competitors treated differently?
Did any private benefit follow the government intervention?
Political benefit or reduced criticism is not enough. The evidence must show interference with a defined government matter or exchange of official action for a private benefit.
XIII. Narrative shielding
UNIKORNAIO characterized the later “government cannot be fixed” narrative as a possible reputational firewall.

A narrative-shielding sequence may include:

promising extraordinary savings and institutional reform;

treating objections as evidence of corruption;

rapidly implementing disruptive changes;

minimizing operational failures;

attributing errors to inherited dysfunction;
withdrawing before full auditing occurs; and
presenting incomplete results as proof that the system defeated reform.
This may be politically relevant. It becomes legally significant only if public messaging contained material deception connected to:
acquisition of money or property;
concealment of conflicts;
obstruction of an investigation;
false statements within federal jurisdiction;

or another legally cognizable scheme.

XIV. “Plausible deniability through dysfunction”

The press release alleged that engineered dysfunction could provide plausible deniability.

Under that theory, responsibility becomes difficult to assign because:

authority is informal;

personnel status is unclear;

instructions are oral or transmitted through private channels;

access is technically granted by host agencies;

decisions are described as recommendations;

multiple agencies implement the action;

and records are fragmented across systems.

Investigators should therefore reconstruct:

formal and practical chains of command;

identity of each decision-maker;
who requested each access grant;
who selected the personnel;
who approved the action;
what communications preceded it;

and who received the resulting benefit.

Informality may complicate proof but does not itself establish criminal purpose.

XV. Negligence, recklessness, and sabotage must remain distinct

The original press release associated national-security risk with possible operational sabotage.

For legal accuracy:
Negligence generally concerns failure to exercise reasonable care.
Gross negligence concerns a more severe departure from ordinary care.
Recklessness generally requires conscious disregard of a substantial risk.
Intentional sabotage requires proof of deliberate destructive or obstructive purpose under the applicable law.
If DOGE staffing created security risk, the conduct might support findings of:
inadequate controls;

negligent supervision;

administrative noncompliance;
gross negligence;
or reckless disregard.
It should not be labeled intentional sabotage without evidence of purpose, knowledge, communications, and actions directed toward producing the harmful result.
XVI. National-security implications
The alleged staffing and access pattern could present national-security risks involving:
Treasury payment infrastructure;

benefits systems;

government employee information;

classified or controlled information;

regulatory investigations;

contractor records;

cybersecurity architecture;
critical infrastructure;
and government-wide identity resolution.
Potential consequences include:

foreign intelligence collection;

insider exploitation;
identity theft;
disruption of payments;
exposure of investigators;
commercial espionage;

manipulation of public records;

and weakening of continuity of government.

A national-security risk assessment is different from a finding that national-security harm occurred.

XVII. Applicable counterintelligence questions

Investigators should determine whether DOGE-associated personnel:
had foreign financial relationships;
held positions in companies with foreign investors;
communicated through foreign-controlled platforms;
accessed export-controlled or intelligence-related records;
traveled or communicated with foreign officials;
retained data after government service;
or failed required security disclosures.
Foreign business relationships do not establish disloyalty or espionage. They determine the level of review, disclosure, mitigation, and monitoring required.

XVIII. Public notice value of the press release

The exhibit may possess evidentiary value by showing that, by December 24, 2025:
UNIKORNAIO publicly articulated the staffing-risk theory;
concerns about access and security were documented;
the obstruction-by-appointment theory was identified;
the “giving up” narrative was interpreted as a possible withdrawal phase;
and government and private actors could be placed on notice of the alleged risks.
Publication does not prove that the recipients actually read the statement.
Actual notice requires:

distribution records;

email receipts;

web analytics;

certified-mail records;

social-media delivery;
acknowledgments;
or subsequent communications referencing the statement.
XIX. Evidentiary use and hearsay limitations
The press release may be offered to establish:
that the statement was made;
its date and contents;

UNIKORNAIO’s contemporaneous understanding;

notice provided to recipients;

the chronology of allegations;

and consistency or evolution of the investigative theory.

It should not automatically be offered for the truth of every embedded allegation.
The attributed Musk quotation presents an additional hearsay and authentication issue unless supported by:
original audio or video;
an authenticated transcript;
Musk’s own post;

an admission;

or reliable contemporaneous reporting.

The strongest version of this exhibit should attach the original press release and separate corroborating sources.
XX. RICO relevance
The press release itself is not a RICO predicate. It is a notice and theory document.
Potential RICO significance depends on independent proof of qualifying acts such as:
bribery connected to appointments or access;
wire or mail fraud involving concealed conflicts;
obstruction of official proceedings;

witness tampering or retaliation;

destruction or concealment of records;

extortion;

or money laundering.
The alleged enterprise theory would require proof that staffing, access, enforcement retreat, and narrative management were coordinated components of an ongoing organization—not merely parallel consequences of aggressive government restructuring.
XXI. Required corroboration
The following evidence would materially strengthen or refute the press release:
original recording or transcript of Musk’s remarks;
authenticated copy of the December 24, 2025 press release;

publication and distribution metadata;

complete DOGE personnel roster;

job descriptions and qualifications;
suitability and clearance records;
financial disclosures and conflicts;
access requests and system privileges;
security training;

rules-of-behavior acknowledgments;

supervisory chains;

inspector-general findings;

adverse security events;

investigations involving affiliated persons or companies;

personnel actions affecting investigators;
case closures and enforcement changes;
communications discussing protective objectives;
records showing interference with evidence or witnesses;
and evidence identifying private benefits following the alleged intervention.
XXII. Counterevidence and alternative explanations
A reliable report must preserve the following:

Musk’s documented December 2025 remarks indicate disappointment and a judgment that DOGE achieved limited results; they do not expressly admit obstruction.

The exact phrase “given up on trying to fix Washington, D.C.” remains an attributed formulation unless authenticated through the original source.

Political appointees may lawfully implement administration policy.

Private-sector personnel may possess valuable technical skills despite limited government experience.

Rapid access may have been motivated by genuine concern about government fraud and antiquated systems.
GAO found no evidence that unintended Treasury modification privileges were used to alter payment data.
Operational mistakes may result from haste, inadequate planning, or negligence rather than deliberate sabotage.
Agency reductions affecting investigators do not prove that a particular investigation was targeted.
Musk’s withdrawal may have reflected statutory limits on special government employment, business obligations, political disagreements, or personal cost.
Public criticism of entrenched bureaucracy is protected political speech.
A narrative benefiting an actor’s reputation does not automatically constitute fraud.
The press release documents UNIKORNAIO’s allegations; it does not independently validate them.

DOGE-42 — Federal Procurement Consolidation, Cancellation of Competing Contracts, Government Data as a Commercial Asset, and the Alleged Steering of Public Technology Infrastructure to DOGE-Connected Firms

I. Investigative purpose

This section examines whether DOGE-associated officials used federal procurement consolidation, contract reviews, cancellations, agency data access, and government-wide technology adoption to advantage politically or professionally connected private companies.

The investigated theory is that the administration:

centralized purchasing authority within GSA and OMB;
canceled or reduced existing contracts;
weakened agency-level procurement personnel and independent review;
promoted rapid adoption of commercial AI, cloud, satellite, analytics, and payment products;
created government-wide purchasing vehicles for a small group of major vendors;
and potentially gave connected companies access to federal workflows, data, and future contracting opportunities.

The public record establishes extensive procurement centralization and rapid commercial-technology adoption. It does not yet establish that a particular contract was criminally steered or that protected government data was transferred to a DOGE-connected company for commercial use.

II. Executive Order 14240

On March 20, 2025, President Trump issued Executive Order 14240, Eliminating Waste and Saving Taxpayer Dollars by Consolidating Procurement. Official Executive Order 14240.

The order stated that the federal government spent approximately $490 billion annually on common goods and services and directed greater consolidation through the General Services Administration.

The order required:

agencies to propose transferring procurement of common goods and services to GSA;
GSA to prepare a government-wide consolidation plan;
OMB to designate GSA as executive agent for government-wide information-technology acquisition contracts; and
GSA to rationalize indefinite-delivery contract vehicles and eliminate duplication.

The order preserved existing statutory authority and required implementation consistent with applicable law.

III. The stated public purpose

The administration identified several facially legitimate purposes:

combining federal purchasing power;
securing lower prices;
reducing duplicate contracts;
standardizing terms;
improving visibility into spending;
simplifying licensing;
preventing agencies from paying different prices for identical products;
and allowing mission agencies to concentrate on their substantive work.

Procurement consolidation can create substantial taxpayer savings.

The investigative concern is not centralization by itself. It is whether centralization placed excessive discretion in the hands of officials with private-sector relationships and reduced the number of meaningful competitive pathways through which smaller vendors could reach federal customers.

IV. The structural change

Before the order, individual departments and subcomponents frequently maintained separate procurement operations and contract vehicles.

The centralized model shifted influence toward:

GSA;
OMB;
the Federal Acquisition Service;
government-wide acquisition-contract administrators;
White House technology officials;
DOGE-associated personnel;
and centralized vendor-management teams.

The resulting structure can be represented as:

flowchart TD
    A["Individual agencies and procurement offices"] --> B["GSA and OMB consolidation"]
    B --> C["Government-wide contract vehicles"]
    C --> D["Selected commercial vendors"]
    D --> E["Technology used across multiple agencies"]
    E --> F["Data, workflow and switching-cost dependence"]
Centralization can lower prices at stages B and C while increasing vendor concentration and switching costs at stages D through F.
V. OneGov strategy
GSA implemented a government-wide purchasing strategy known as OneGov.
By the end of 2025, GSA reported completing 19 agreements with major technology companies, including:
Adobe;
Amazon Web Services;
Anthropic;

Box;

DocuSign;

Elastic;

Google;

Microsoft;
OpenAI;
Oracle;
Perplexity;
Slack;
ServiceNow; and
xAI.
GSA described OneGov as a method of acting as one buyer, negotiating large discounts, creating standardized licensing, and forming direct relationships with original equipment manufacturers. GSA OneGov update.
This vendor diversity is significant counterevidence against the allegation that OneGov was created exclusively for one DOGE-connected company.
VI. OneGov eligibility and incumbent advantage
GSA’s initial OneGov tiers prioritized vendors based on existing federal spending and deployment:
TierAnnual federal spendingAgency presenceTier 1More than $500 millionAll 24 CFO Act agenciesTier 2$200–$500 millionAt least 18 agenciesTier 3$50–$200 millionAt least 10 agenciesTier 4$10–$50 millionAt least 5 agencies
These criteria reward established federal-market penetration.
The model may produce immediate discounts, but it can also disadvantage:

startups;

regional vendors;

small businesses;

open-source providers;
Tribal enterprises;
firms lacking FedRAMP authorization;
and companies without preexisting agency relationships.
The investigative question is whether the tier system measured legitimate security and scale requirements or converted past federal dominance into preferential access to future government-wide markets.
VII. Direct-vendor relationships
GSA stated that OneGov sought direct relationships with manufacturers rather than reliance on resellers.
This can eliminate hidden markups and produce clearer pricing. It can also reduce opportunities for:
small-business resellers;
integrators;
service-disabled-veteran-owned companies;
minority-owned contractors;
local support companies;
and specialized implementation firms.
Investigators should determine whether GSA complied with:
small-business set-aside requirements;
socioeconomic contracting programs;
subcontracting-plan obligations;
competition requirements;

and statutory goals applicable to small federal contractors.

VIII. Contract cancellation program

DOGE publicly claimed savings from canceling or reducing contracts involving:
consulting;
diversity and inclusion programs;
communications;
training;
software;
real estate;

foreign assistance;

research;

subscriptions;

and other services.

A termination for convenience may be legally permissible even when the contractor did nothing wrong. It does not establish fraud or contractual breach by the terminated vendor.
For each cancellation, investigators should determine:
What services were being provided?
How much had already been obligated and paid?
What termination costs were incurred?
Were the claimed savings gross or net?

Was the work actually discontinued?

Was it transferred to federal employees?
Was it transferred to a different contractor?
Did a DOGE-connected firm receive the replacement work?
Did service disruption create additional costs?
Was the original contract competitively awarded?

The strongest steering evidence would be a canceled contract followed by a substantially similar award to a connected company without lawful competition.

IX. Reliability of claimed savings

Public DOGE contract-cancellation figures should not be accepted without independent reconciliation.
The reported value of a canceled contract may differ from actual savings because:
the contract ceiling may never have been fully funded;
prior expenditures cannot be recovered;
agencies may incur termination costs;
another contractor may perform the same work;
federal employees may absorb the work;
service disruption may create downstream losses;
or a contract entry may be duplicated or incorrectly described.
Required records include:

contract ceilings;

obligated amounts;

invoices;
payments;
termination settlements;
replacement contracts;
and actual agency budget reductions.
X. FAA, Verizon, and Starlink concerns
Public reporting in February 2025 alleged that officials were considering replacing or reducing Verizon’s role in a multibillion-dollar Federal Aviation Administration telecommunications-modernization contract while SpaceX personnel and Starlink equipment were being introduced at the FAA.
The reported circumstances raised an apparent conflict because:
Musk exercised substantial influence over DOGE;
SpaceX owns Starlink;

the FAA regulates SpaceX;

and Starlink could benefit from federal telecommunications work.

The reporting did not establish that the entire Verizon contract was ultimately transferred to Starlink. Washington Post report.

The defensible allegation is therefore that a potential replacement or parallel deployment required conflict and procurement review—not that a completed $2.4 billion contract transfer has been proved.

XI. SpaceX and Starlink’s existing federal position
SpaceX and Starlink already held substantial government relationships before DOGE, including work involving:
NASA launches;
military launches;
satellite communications;
national-security space programs;
disaster communications;

and other federal services.

Preexisting contracts should not be treated as products of DOGE.
The relevant analysis is whether DOGE-era actions:
increased contract value;
eliminated competitors;
waived competition;
modified requirements to favor SpaceX;
transferred confidential competitor information;

altered FAA or other regulatory decisions;

or created new sole-source opportunities.

XII. xAI’s government-wide agreement

On September 25, 2025, GSA announced an agreement making xAI’s Grok models available to federal agencies for $0.42 per agency for 18 months.
The agreement included:
access to Grok models;
dedicated xAI engineers;

training;

custom implementation assistance;

and an upgrade path to more extensive enterprise subscriptions.

GSA described xAI as the final major frontier-model provider added to its suite, not the sole provider. GSA–xAI announcement.

The relationship creates an apparent conflict because Musk was closely associated with DOGE and later received a government-wide pathway for a company he controlled.
The nominal price does not eliminate the conflict inquiry.
XIII. Introductory pricing and future dependence
The $0.42 price applied to an introductory period and limited offering. Future enterprise use could require paid upgrades, additional capacity, security authorizations, implementation work, or cloud infrastructure.
Low introductory pricing may be legitimate customer acquisition. It can also create:
workflow dependence;

retraining costs;

proprietary integrations;

data migration costs;
user familiarity;
custom agents;
technical lock-in;
and future paid demand.
A predatory-pricing theory would require evidence that the price was below an appropriate measure of cost, was designed to exclude competitors, and created a dangerous probability of recoupment.
No such finding follows from the promotional price alone.

XIV. Competing AI agreements

GSA made other frontier AI products available on similarly favorable terms:

OpenAI’s ChatGPT Enterprise was offered for approximately $1 per participating agency for one year. GSA–OpenAI agreement.

Anthropic offered Claude Enterprise and Claude for Government for approximately $1 per eligible agency. GSA–Anthropic agreement.
Google offered Gemini for Government at approximately $0.47 per agency, together with cloud and agentic capabilities. GSA–Google agreement.
This multi-vendor structure weighs against an allegation that xAI received an exclusive federal AI monopoly.
Potential preferential treatment could still exist in:
contract duration;

usage limits;

engineering access;

data permissions;

security approvals;

promotional treatment;

integration with agency systems;

or future upgrade terms.
XV. Dedicated engineers inside government workflows
The xAI agreement included dedicated engineers to assist federal implementation. Other AI agreements also included technical assistance.
Embedded commercial engineers may gain valuable knowledge about:
agency workflows;
procurement requirements;
data structures;
operational bottlenecks;

compliance standards;

user behavior;

future purchasing needs;

and competitor products.

This knowledge can legitimately be used to provide contracted services. It becomes problematic if:
engineers access data outside contractual scope;
government information is used to train commercial models without authorization;

procurement-sensitive information reaches the vendor;

competitor information is disclosed;

or embedded personnel help draft future requirements favoring their own company.
XVI. Government data as a commercial asset
Federal data may include:
payment transactions;
benefits records;
healthcare information;
tax information;
personnel files;

procurement pricing;

contractor-performance records;

regulatory filings;

investigation material;
cybersecurity information;
geospatial data;
and operational communications.
Such data can improve private commercial products by enabling:
model training;
product testing;
market analysis;

competitor assessment;

fraud-detection development;
customer targeting;
workflow optimization;
and identification of future government demand.
The government’s possession of data does not authorize its unrestricted commercial use.

XVII. DOGE’s system access

DOGE personnel obtained or sought access to sensitive systems across multiple agencies, including Treasury payment systems and administrative, personnel, and program data.

Litigation brought by state attorneys general alleged that DOGE and Musk exercised unauthorized authority over:
sensitive data;
federal payments;
staffing;
agencies;
and contracts.
The litigation establishes that states formally challenged this conduct. It does not itself prove that DOGE transferred data to private companies. Associated Press summary of the state challenge.
The crucial missing evidence is what DOGE personnel did with the information after access.
XVIII. Contractor and competitor information
Treasury and federal procurement systems may contain information concerning companies that compete with Musk-affiliated or other DOGE-connected firms, including:
payment amounts;
pricing;

subcontractors;

revenue dependence;
agency contacts;
performance disputes;
contract renewal dates;
technical specifications;
and internal government evaluations.
Access to such information could provide a material competitive advantage.
Investigators must determine whether access controls prevented:

Musk;

private company employees;

DOGE special government employees;

consultants;

or outside systems
from receiving or using this information for nongovernmental purposes.
No public evidence presently proves that Musk used Treasury payment data to target competitors commercially.
XIX. AI training and government information
AI vendors should be required to disclose whether federal prompts, documents, outputs, or metadata are:

retained;

used for model training;

reviewed by employees;

combined with commercial-user data;

transferred to subprocessors;
stored outside government environments;
or made available for product development.
Contract terms should require clear answers concerning:
data ownership;
training restrictions;
deletion;
audit rights;
breach notification;

model isolation;

classified and controlled-unclassified information;

and post-contract portability.
An allegation that federal data was used to train a private model requires logs or contractual evidence, not merely proof that federal employees used the product.
XX. Cloud and data-lock-in risks
An AI application commonly depends on an underlying cloud environment. Once an agency builds:
databases;

retrieval systems;

authentication;

custom agents;

workflows;

and staff practices
around one provider, migration becomes expensive.
Potential anticompetitive mechanisms include:
nonportable data formats;
high egress charges;
proprietary application interfaces;
nontransferable security approvals;

exclusive model hosting;

long-term volume commitments;
and technical requirements written around an incumbent product.
Centralized procurement can magnify these effects because one contractual decision may shape adoption throughout the federal government.
XXI. Palantir and centralized data architecture
Palantir and similar data-integration contractors possess extensive federal experience and the ability to combine information from multiple sources.
Their platforms may provide genuine operational value in:
defense;
intelligence;

immigration;

healthcare;

fraud detection;

logistics;
and program administration.
The antitrust and civil-liberties concern arises when one contractor becomes the connective layer across agencies, making replacement difficult and allowing it to acquire unparalleled knowledge of federal systems.
Investigators should identify:
all DOGE-era Palantir awards and modifications;
whether Palantir personnel worked with DOGE;

whether competing analytics platforms were considered;

what data was integrated;

which agencies could query the combined information;
and whether any private model or product was improved using government data.
A relationship should not be alleged solely from ideological or venture-capital connections.
XXII. Contract steering
A classic steering allegation requires more than proof that a connected company received a contract.
Relevant indicators include:
specifications written around one vendor;

unexplained sole-source awards;

unusually short bid periods;

advance information given to the favored firm;

disqualification of technically qualified competitors;

cancellation followed by substantially similar replacement work;
suppressed evaluator objections;
retroactive justification;
pricing inconsistent with market value;
and intervention by a conflicted official.
Evidence of competitive bidding, independent scoring, market research, and documented recusal would materially weaken the allegation.
XXIII. Organizational conflicts of interest

Federal Acquisition Regulation provisions address situations where a contractor’s other interests may:

impair objectivity;

create unequal access to information;
or allow the contractor to write requirements for work it later performs.
Three recurring forms are especially relevant:
A. Unequal access to information
A company receives confidential information unavailable to competitors.
B. Biased ground rules

A company helps draft specifications or procurement rules favoring its own product.

C. Impaired objectivity

A contractor evaluates its own products, affiliates, or competitors.

Contracting officers must identify, evaluate, avoid, neutralize, or mitigate organizational conflicts as required by applicable procurement rules.

XXIV. Personal conflicts of interest
Officials participating in contract or cancellation decisions may have conflicts involving:
ownership interests;
former employers;
prospective employment;
family interests;
venture-capital investments;
business partners;
creditors;
or major political benefactors.

For DOGE-associated personnel, investigators should obtain:

financial disclosures;

ethics agreements;

recusals;
waivers;
certificates of divestiture;

screening arrangements;

and communications concerning particular matters.

A broad policy affecting an entire industry may be treated differently from participation in a contract specifically involving an official’s company.

XXV. Competition in Contracting Act

The Competition in Contracting Act generally requires full and open competition unless a statutory exception applies.

Potential exceptions include:

only one responsible source;

unusual and compelling urgency;

national security;

authorized statutory programs;

and other specifically defined circumstances.
Urgency caused by agency delay or political preference should not be used automatically to bypass competition.
Any sole-source or limited-competition award should be supported by:
written justification;
required approvals;
market research;
price analysis;
and publication where legally required.

XXVI. Procurement Integrity Act

The Procurement Integrity Act restricts:
disclosure of contractor bid or proposal information;
disclosure of source-selection information;
receipt of protected procurement information;
and certain employment contacts involving procurement officials.
Potential violations could arise if DOGE personnel or private-company employees obtained competitors’:
bids;

technical proposals;

pricing;

cost structures;

or source-selection evaluations.

Proof requires identification of the information, its protected status, the recipient, knowledge, and use.
XXVII. Trade Secrets Act and confidentiality
The Trade Secrets Act, 18 U.S.C. § 1905, restricts unauthorized disclosure by federal personnel of certain confidential commercial and financial information.
Relevant material may include:
contractor pricing;

proprietary technology;

customer information;

business strategy;
manufacturing processes;
and confidential financial data.
An unlawful-disclosure theory requires evidence that protected information was actually disclosed without legal authorization. Access within government is not necessarily disclosure.
XXVIII. Privacy Act and personal data

When procurement or AI systems process records about identifiable individuals, the Privacy Act may require:

a lawful system of records;

published notices;
authorized uses;
access controls;
data minimization;

and restrictions on disclosure.

Potentially affected records include:
Social Security files;
benefits information;
federal employment records;
payment information;

and healthcare data.

Privacy violations are not automatically antitrust violations, but misuse of protected data could produce unlawful commercial advantage and support separate causes of action.

XXIX. Bid-rigging and procurement-fraud theories

Potential criminal conduct would include:

competitors agreeing who will win;
sham bids;
inflated bids accompanied by kickbacks;
false small-business status;
concealed subcontractors;
false cybersecurity certifications;

manipulated performance data;

or fraudulent savings claims used to justify a replacement award.

Bid rigging may be prosecuted criminally under Sherman Act §1. Procurement fraud may also implicate:

wire fraud;
false statements;
the False Claims Act;
bribery;
gratuities;
conspiracy;

and money laundering.

XXX. False Claims Act implications
A contractor may face False Claims Act exposure if it knowingly submits materially false claims concerning:
prices;
labor;
cybersecurity;

data isolation;

domestic sourcing;

small-business eligibility;

product capability;
FedRAMP compliance;
or use of government information.
The statute provides civil remedies and permits certain whistleblower actions.
Ordinary nonperformance or contractual disagreement does not automatically constitute fraud.
XXXI. Antitrust implications
Procurement consolidation may create monopsony power because the federal government acts as a unified buyer.
Monopsony can lower prices, but it can also:

reduce the number of suppliers;

force smaller vendors from the market;
favor firms capable of accepting short-term losses;
and leave the government dependent on a few surviving platforms.
Potential antitrust theories include:
predatory bidding;
exclusive dealing;
tying;

unlawful information exchange;

monopolization of specialized federal technology markets;

and acquisition of competitors made vulnerable by canceled contracts.
Government purchasing decisions are subject to specialized doctrines and immunities. The precise legal vehicle may be procurement protest, administrative review, False Claims Act litigation, or constitutional challenge rather than conventional private antitrust litigation.

DOGE-19 — FIT21, the CLARITY Act, Stablecoin Legislation, and the Alleged Conversion of Campaign Spending into Statutory Safe Harbors

I. Scope and investigative purpose

This exhibit examines whether the cryptocurrency political-financing network described in DOGE-18 converted electoral expenditures, lobbying pressure, and campaign support into legislation providing favorable jurisdictional classifications, exemptions, provisional operating authority, federal preemption, or limitations on enforcement.

The principal legislative vehicles are:

H.R. 4763 — Financial Innovation and Technology for the 21st Century Act (“FIT21”);
H.R. 3633 — Digital Asset Market Clarity Act of 2025 (“CLARITY Act”);
S. 1582 — GENIUS Act, governing payment stablecoins; and
Related amendments, agency rules, appropriations provisions, and executive policies implementing or expanding those measures.

The operative allegation is not that favorable legislation is inherently corrupt. The investigation concerns whether industry participants obtained statutory benefits through prohibited coordination, concealed conflicts, bribery, undisclosed foreign influence, materially deceptive lobbying, or an agreement linking electoral assistance to official action.

II. Legislative chronology

A. FIT21

FIT21 passed the House of Representatives during the 118th Congress on May 22, 2024, by a vote of 279–136. It was received in the Senate but was not enacted before that Congress expired.

FIT21 nevertheless established the policy architecture later incorporated into the CLARITY Act:

Separating a digital asset from the investment contract through which it was sold;

Moving substantial spot-market jurisdiction to the CFTC;
Creating a “mature blockchain system” certification process;
Establishing disclosure-based exemptions from securities registration;
Excluding certain decentralized-finance activities;
Protecting some software developers and blockchain-service providers;
Providing transitional registration arrangements; and
Limiting the application of state securities-registration laws.
Then-SEC Chair Gary Gensler opposed FIT21, warning that it could create regulatory gaps, allow crypto enterprises to self-certify outside SEC oversight, and potentially permit investment contracts to escape established securities protections. SEC statement on FIT21

The enrolled House text is preserved in the Government Publishing Office’s FIT21 record.

B. CLARITY Act

H.R. 3633 was introduced on May 29, 2025. The House passed it on July 17, 2025. A substantially amended version was reported in the Senate on June 1, 2026 and referred to the Senate Committee on Banking, Housing, and Urban Affairs.

As of the date of this exhibit, the available legislative record identifies the measure as a reported Senate bill—not enacted law. Current GPO legislative record

C. GENIUS Act

The GENIUS Act established a federal framework for payment stablecoins. President Donald J. Trump signed S. 1582 into law on July 18, 2025. Presidential signing record

Unlike FIT21 and the pending CLARITY framework, the GENIUS Act is enacted legislation. Investigative findings must therefore distinguish:

Proposed statutory benefits;

House-passed provisions;
Senate amendments;
Final enacted provisions; and
Benefits created through later agency implementation.
III. The alleged campaign-finance-to-legislation sequence

The public record establishes the following sequence:

PeriodDevelopment2023Fairshake formed; major cryptocurrency companies began funding it2023–2024Coinbase, Ripple, a16z, and associated interests financed electoral and advocacy operationsMay 2024FIT21 passed the House2024 electionFairshake and affiliates supported or opposed candidates based substantially on crypto-policy alignmentJanuary 2025New administration adopted expressly pro-cryptocurrency policyFebruary–May 2025Major SEC cryptocurrency cases were dismissed or withdrawnMay 2025CLARITY Act introducedJuly 2025House passed CLARITY; GENIUS Act signed into law2025–2026Fairshake maintained a nine-figure political-financing operationJune 2026Amended CLARITY legislation reported in the Senate

This chronology supports an investigation into influence and regulatory capture. It does not, without communications or other evidence of an exchange, establish that campaign expenditures purchased particular votes or statutory provisions.
IV. Statutory-benefit map
The proposed CLARITY framework contains both regulatory obligations and substantial forms of relief.
ProvisionRegulatory functionPotential private benefit§§101–104Defines digital commodities and related termsDetermines which assets escape full securities regulation§106Expedited registration and provisional statusAllows continued operation while final rules remain incomplete§109Protects certain non-controlling developers and service providersLimits money-transmitter treatment§201Separates a digital commodity from an investment contractRestricts continuing securities-law treatment§202Exempts qualifying primary transactionsAvoids traditional securities registration§203Excludes qualifying secondary transactions and staking rewardsReduces securities-law exposure for exchanges and token holders§§204–205Insider-sale rules and mature-system certificationReduces restrictions after maturity certification§301Excludes digital commodities and permitted payment stablecoins from “security” statusRemoves assets from major portions of SEC jurisdiction§308Treats digital commodities as covered securitiesPreempts specified state registration requirements§§309 and 409Excludes identified DeFi activitiesProtects developers, interfaces, wallets, and validators from parts of SEC/CFTC regulation§310Limits balance-sheet treatment of customer digital assetsBenefits bank custody operations§401Gives the CFTC exclusive regulatory jurisdiction over defined spot-market activityConsolidates oversight under a commodity-oriented regulator§403Permits exchange-driven listing certificationAllows regulated platforms to initiate asset eligibility§405Recognizes certain state and foreign custodiansExpands potential qualified-custodian pathways§§408, 413Authorizes exemptions and conflict rulesLeaves significant questions to later agency discretion
This summary derives from the House Financial Services Committee’s section-by-section CLARITY analysis. The final Senate version must be analyzed separately because amendments may add, remove, or materially alter these protections.
V. Separation of the token from the investment contract
One of the most consequential provisions is the statutory separation of a digital asset from the investment contract through which it was initially offered or sold.
Under this approach:
A fundraising transaction may constitute an investment contract;
The token delivered through that transaction may separately qualify as a digital commodity; and
Later secondary-market sales may fall outside the original securities transaction.
This structure potentially benefits:
Token issuers facing continuing securities-law exposure;
Exchanges accused of operating unregistered securities markets;
Venture-capital investors holding large token allocations;
Market makers facilitating secondary trading;
Custodians and staking-service providers; and
Insiders seeking liquidity after maturity or lockup conditions are satisfied.
The policy rationale is that a tradable asset should not permanently remain a security merely because an earlier transaction constituted an investment contract.
The countervailing concern is that promoters could raise capital through securities transactions, build a secondary market, and then obtain commodity treatment before investors receive protections comparable to those applicable to conventional public companies.
VI. “Mature blockchain” certification
The mature-blockchain process is central because maturity can reduce disclosure obligations and restrictions on insider sales.
The investigation should determine:
Whether certification depends excessively on issuer-supplied information;
Whether the SEC has sufficient time and resources to challenge a certification;

Whether nominal decentralization can conceal coordinated control;

Whether affiliated foundations, validators, market makers, and governance delegates are aggregated;

Whether token holdings are evaluated by beneficial ownership rather than separate wallet addresses;

Whether venture-capital agreements create off-chain control;
Whether insiders can transfer control to affiliated entities before certification; and
Whether false certification creates an explicit private and governmental cause of action.
A mature-system designation should not operate as retroactive absolution for prior fraud, manipulation, unregistered offerings, sanctions violations, or illicit financing.
VII. Provisional registration and grandfathering risk
Section 106 of the House framework contemplated expedited registration and provisional status. During that period, an applicant could continue listing assets already available before registration, subject to a joint SEC–CFTC delisting mechanism.
This creates a possible first-mover advantage for large incumbent exchanges:
Previously listed assets may remain available during rulemaking;
Smaller competitors may face higher entry costs;
Regulators must affirmatively identify and delist problematic assets;
Existing market liquidity may become a practical presumption of legitimacy; and
Historical listing misconduct may be separated from future registration eligibility.
Investigators should determine whether Coinbase or other Fairshake contributors sought language protecting their existing listings or limiting the consequences of prior conduct.
A defensible transitional system should expressly state that provisional status:
Does not legalize prior violations;
Does not bar pending investigations;
Does not create a presumption that an asset is lawful;
Can be revoked immediately for material omissions;
Requires disclosure of prior regulatory warnings; and
Preserves restitution, disgorgement, and victim claims.
VIII. Developer and DeFi exclusions
Sections 109, 309, and 409 protect categories of non-controlling developers, validators, wallet developers, user-interface providers, and other infrastructure participants.
These protections respond to legitimate concerns that a person who merely publishes software should not automatically be treated as a bank, broker, exchange, or money transmitter.
The potential loophole arises when formally “non-controlling” participants collectively exercise functional control through:
Administrative keys;
Front-end access;
Fee switches;
Governance-token concentration;
Validator coordination;
Oracle selection;
Upgrade authority;
Transaction ordering;
Blacklisting or whitelisting;
Treasury control; or
Coordinated affiliated entities.
The correct investigative question is therefore not merely whether one entity possesses unilateral legal control. It is whether the person or coordinated group possesses practical power to operate, modify, profit from, restrict, or terminate the financial service.
Legislation that immunizes labels instead of examining functional activity could create a statutory channel for money laundering, sanctions evasion, terrorist financing, trafficking proceeds, and unregistered financial intermediation.
IX. AML and illicit-finance coverage
The House CLARITY framework would classify registered digital-commodity exchanges, brokers, and dealers as financial institutions under the Bank Secrecy Act. It contemplates:
AML and counter-terrorist-financing programs;
Transaction records;
Suspicious-activity monitoring;
Customer-identification programs; and
Compliance with U.S. sanctions.
Those are material safeguards.
However, the legislation also risks leaving activity outside the regulated perimeter when services are divided among developers, interfaces, autonomous protocols, validators, offshore entities, and nominally noncustodial operators.
An adequate framework should apply AML responsibilities based on risk-producing financial activity and functional control—not merely custody or corporate form. It should prevent participants from deliberately fragmenting operations so that no single entity meets the statutory definition.
X. State-law preemption and fragmented enforcement
Section 308 would classify digital commodities as “covered securities” for specified purposes, limiting state blue-sky registration requirements while purportedly preserving state antifraud authority concerning securities.
Questions remain concerning:
Whether a state can act when the defendant asserts the asset is a digital commodity rather than a security;
Whether state commodity, consumer-protection, gambling, money-transmission, and deceptive-practices statutes remain intact;

Whether local prosecutors can obtain transaction and beneficial-ownership records;

Whether federal classification decisions preclude state litigation;

Whether states retain authority over mixed transactions;

Whether tribal regulators retain concurrent jurisdiction; and

Whether victims possess a private cause of action.

A federal framework that removes state registration authority without providing equally strong federal resources may create an enforcement vacuum.
Preserving state antifraud authority only over “securities” is inadequate if the legislation simultaneously removes most covered tokens from the securities category.
XI. Stablecoin legislation and private monetary power

The GENIUS Act provides federal legitimacy and a regulatory pathway for private payment-stablecoin issuers. Properly regulated stablecoins may improve settlement speed, payment efficiency, and dollar accessibility.

They can also create concentrated private power over:
Dollar-denominated payment infrastructure;
Customer transaction data;
Reserve-asset demand;
Wallet access and blacklisting;
Cross-border settlement;

Treasury-market purchases;

Merchant payment systems; and

Programmable financial restrictions.

The necessary investigative questions include:

Who owns or controls the issuer;

Who receives reserve income;

Which banks, custodians, and exchanges receive related business;
Whether reserves are segregated and bankruptcy-remote;
Whether redemptions are enforceable;
Whether foreign issuers receive equivalent treatment without equivalent supervision;
Whether stablecoins are used to conceal beneficial ownership;
Whether political officials or their families have issuer interests; and
Whether enforcement can reach affiliated offshore entities.
XII. World Liberty Financial and presidential conflicts

The GENIUS Act must be examined together with World Liberty Financial and its USD1 stablecoin, addressed in DOGE-14.

The potential conflict is structural:

The President sponsored a pro-cryptocurrency policy agenda;

A business associated with his family participated in the digital-asset market;

Stablecoin legislation created federal recognition and commercial opportunity for qualifying private issuers;

Executive agencies obtained substantial discretion over implementation; and
The value of an affiliated enterprise could be affected by federal policy.
The existence of this conflict does not itself prove that the GENIUS Act was enacted for World Liberty Financial. It does require scrutiny of:
Beneficial ownership;
Revenue-allocation agreements;

Foreign token purchasers;

Stablecoin reserves and custodians;

White House and legislative communications;
Participation in drafting or amendment negotiations;
Ethics opinions and recusals;
Agency contacts; and
Whether presidential-family interests received treatment unavailable to competitors.
Any ethics restriction that applies to members of Congress but excludes the President, Vice President, senior executive officials, spouses, dependent children, or controlled entities creates a serious appearance and enforcement gap.

XIII. Coinbase’s potential statutory benefits

Coinbase could benefit from a market-structure framework through:

Reduced risk that exchange-listed tokens are treated as securities;

CFTC-centered regulation of digital-commodity spot markets;

Provisional registration;

Continued listing of existing assets during implementation;
Express recognition of staking and blockchain services;
Bank custody expansion;
Federal displacement of inconsistent state registration rules;
Clearer stablecoin trading treatment; and
Reduced exposure to the legal theory underlying the SEC’s dismissed enforcement case.
Coinbase’s political spending and advocacy establish motive to obtain these outcomes. They do not prove that legislators agreed to provide them in exchange for Fairshake support.
The decisive evidence would include draft-language transmission, nonpublic commitments, candidate communications, legislative asks linked to electoral support, and records showing donor participation in selecting or revising specific provisions.
XIV. A16z’s potential statutory benefits
Andreessen Horowitz manages investments across exchanges, protocols, wallet providers, infrastructure companies, and token projects.
It could obtain portfolio-wide benefits from:

Developer exclusions;

DeFi exclusions;

Secondary-market commodity treatment;

Maturity certifications;

Reduced registration obligations;

Expanded token liquidity;
Insider-sale pathways;
Limits on state securities regulation; and
Increased institutional custody and banking participation.
A16z publicly connected its Fairshake contributions with representing entrepreneurs in its portfolio and advancing favorable rules.

The investigation should compare its portfolio with each provision and identify:

Companies directly benefiting;

Token holdings becoming more liquid;

Pending or threatened enforcement matters;

A16z-proposed legislative language;

Meetings with bill sponsors;

Contributions preceding legislative action; and

Portfolio valuation changes following votes or regulatory announcements.
XV. Ripple’s potential statutory benefits
Ripple’s business and XRP-related interests could benefit from clearer separation between:
An investment-contract transaction;
The underlying digital asset; and
Later secondary-market trading.
Ripple also benefits from legislation limiting the SEC’s authority over qualifying digital commodities and transferring spot-market regulation to the CFTC.

Ripple’s publicly announced $50 million in Fairshake contributions provides evidence of substantial political investment. It does not establish an unlawful exchange.

Investigators should compare:

Ripple’s litigation positions;

Proposed statutory definitions;

Lobbying submissions;

Draft legislative language;

Fairshake-supported candidates;
Subsequent votes; and
Governmental decisions affecting XRP markets.
XVI. Missing safeguards and potential statutory omissions
The legislative framework should be tested for the following omissions:
A. No anti-amnesty clause
The law should expressly preserve liability for pre-enactment conduct, including fraud, manipulation, unregistered offerings, money laundering, sanctions violations, and false statements.
B. Incomplete concurrent enforcement

Federal, state, tribal, and local authorities should retain concurrent authority over fraud, laundering, trafficking proceeds, consumer harm, and market manipulation.

C. Limited private remedies
Victims may lack a clear federal private cause of action for false maturity certifications, reserve misrepresentations, conflicted listings, or misuse of customer assets.
D. Weak capital-flight reporting
The framework does not comprehensively require reporting of large-scale conversions of domestic capital into offshore or pseudonymous digital assets.
E. Functional-control gaps
Developers and DeFi participants may escape regulation despite possessing practical or collective control.
F. Foreign-influence gaps
The legislation requires studies but may not impose immediate, comprehensive disclosure of foreign beneficial ownership, governance, financing, or lobbying.
G. Ethics gaps

Conflict restrictions should cover the President, Vice President, cabinet officials, senior regulators, members of Congress, immediate family, trusts, controlled entities, and token-based compensation.

H. Listing conflicts

Exchanges should not determine listing eligibility for assets in which the exchange, its executives, investors, lenders, or affiliates possess undisclosed interests.

I. Insufficient insider lockups

Wallet fragmentation, derivatives, loans, affiliated market makers, and offshore transactions can defeat nominal lockup and volume restrictions.
J. Study in place of enforcement
Mandated studies of terrorism, foreign adversaries, NFTs, and DeFi do not substitute for immediate investigative jurisdiction or interim safeguards.
XVII. Regulatory capture versus legitimate policy change
The following evidence would support legitimate policy development:

Public hearings involving consumer, academic, enforcement, and industry witnesses;

Independent legislative analysis;
Disclosed lobbying contacts;
Bipartisan sponsorship based on established policy positions;
Strong antifraud, AML, reserve, and conflict protections;
Preservation of prior liabilities;
Adequately funded regulators; and
Transparent agency rulemaking.
The following evidence would support a regulatory-capture inference:
Industry authorship concealed from the public;

Provisions copied from donor submissions without independent analysis;

Votes or amendments linked to Fairshake support;

Threats of electoral opposition for refusing particular language;

Preferential access for major contributors;

Suppression of law-enforcement objections;
Exemptions tailored to contributors’ pending cases;
Retroactive protection of earlier misconduct;
Ethics exclusions benefiting public officials;
Deliberately underfunded enforcement; or
False public claims about the effect of statutory language.
XVIII. Potential RICO and predicate-act analysis
Legislative advocacy, campaign contributions, and voting are not RICO predicates.
A criminal or civil RICO theory would require an enterprise and a pattern of independently indictable racketeering activity. Potential predicates could arise only if evidence establishes conduct such as:

Bribery involving a payment or benefit exchanged for an official act;

Honest-services wire fraud based on bribery or kickbacks;

Wire fraud involving intentionally false lobbying representations or concealed payment arrangements;

Money laundering involving the proceeds of specified unlawful activity;

Illegal foreign-national contributions;

Straw-donor schemes accompanied by qualifying fraud or laundering offenses;

Obstruction of investigations;
Destruction or falsification of records; or
Witness tampering.
The legislation itself cannot constitute the predicate act. The actionable conduct would be the corrupt agreement, payment, concealment, fraudulent communication, or obstruction used to obtain or protect the statutory result.
XIX. DOGE nexus
A DOGE-specific theory remains contingent upon proof that DOGE personnel or associated private actors:
Participated in drafting or promoting these provisions;
Targeted SEC, CFTC, FinCEN, DOJ, CFPB, or banking personnel who opposed industry demands;
Reduced investigative or enforcement capacity;

Obtained government data useful to cryptocurrency companies;

Coordinated with Fairshake donors;

Recommended appointments tied to the lobbying network;
Suppressed analysis of capital flight or illicit finance;
Redirected scrutiny toward USAID while minimizing digital-asset channels;
Used government reorganization to obstruct pending cases; or
Received political or financial benefits connected to the legislation.
The timing of DOGE-related agency disruption and cryptocurrency enforcement reversals warrants investigation, but timing alone does not establish agreement or causation.
XX. Exculpatory evidence and alternative explanations

A balanced record must acknowledge that:

FIT21 and CLARITY received bipartisan support;

Jurisdictional uncertainty existed before Fairshake was created;

The CFTC already possessed antifraud authority over commodity spot markets;
The SEC’s application of securities law to digital assets generated genuine legal disagreement;
Developer protections may safeguard constitutionally protected software publication;

Stablecoin regulation can be more protective than an unregulated market;

CLARITY imposes registration, custody, disclosure, segregation, AML, and conflict obligations;

Several provisions expressly preserve antifraud authority;

Candidate positions may have preceded industry support;
Independent expenditures may have remained legally independent; and
Favorable legislation does not prove that any legislator accepted a bribe.

