DOGE-40 — Changpeng Zhao’s Presidential Pardon, Binance’s Support for USD1, Prior Anti-Money-Laundering Violations, and the Alleged Exchange of Clemency for Commercial Benefit

I. Investigative purpose

This section examines whether President Donald Trump’s pardon of Binance founder Changpeng “CZ” Zhao was:

an independent exercise of constitutional clemency based on the administration’s opposition to prior cryptocurrency enforcement;
a policy-driven benefit provided to a major digital-asset entrepreneur; or
part of an alleged corrupt exchange involving Binance’s support for World Liberty Financial’s USD1 stablecoin and the resulting financial benefits to Trump-affiliated interests.

The public record establishes a highly significant sequence of commercial and governmental benefits. It does not, without communications or transaction evidence, establish that Binance or Zhao purchased the pardon.

The investigation must separately analyze:

Zhao’s individual conviction and pardon;
Binance’s corporate guilty plea;
the SEC’s civil enforcement case;
Binance’s compliance-monitor obligations;
World Liberty Financial’s commercial arrangements;
USD1’s integration into Binance;
MGX’s $2 billion investment;
and any communications concerning clemency.

II. Binance’s prior criminal conduct

On November 21, 2023, Binance Holdings Limited pleaded guilty to federal violations involving:

the Bank Secrecy Act;
failure to register as a money-transmitting business;
United States sanctions;
anti-money-laundering requirements; and
transactions involving prohibited jurisdictions.

Binance agreed to a financial resolution exceeding $4 billion.

The Justice Department stated that Binance became the world’s largest cryptocurrency exchange in part by prioritizing growth and profit over compliance. Official DOJ Binance resolution.

According to the admitted facts, Binance:

deliberately avoided comprehensive know-your-customer controls;
failed to monitor transactions adequately;
failed for years to file suspicious-activity reports;
retained valuable United States customers while purporting to exclude them;
allowed customers in sanctioned jurisdictions to transact with United States users;
and generated substantial revenue through conduct violating United States law.

These are adjudicated or admitted facts, not merely allegations.

III. National-security and illicit-finance consequences

DOJ and Treasury stated that Binance’s compliance failures facilitated transactions connected to:

sanctioned jurisdictions;
ransomware;
darknet markets;
cybercriminals;
narcotics;
child exploitation;
terrorist financing;
fraud;
and other illicit activity.

Treasury imposed major FinCEN and OFAC resolutions and required extensive remediation. Official Treasury settlement.

The record is directly relevant to the broader DOGE allegation because the administration publicly focused on alleged waste, fraud, foreign aid, and terrorism financing while simultaneously providing relief to a digital-asset executive whose company admitted serious failures in the same illicit-finance domain.

That inconsistency supports investigation. It does not prove that DOGE controlled the pardon.

IV. Zhao’s individual conviction

Zhao pleaded guilty to violating the Bank Secrecy Act by failing to maintain an effective anti-money-laundering program at Binance.

Material facts include:

Zhao founded and controlled Binance;
he knew the exchange served United States customers;
he knew an effective AML program could reduce customer growth and revenue;
he prioritized business expansion over compliance;
he resigned as Binance CEO;
he paid a $50 million criminal fine; and
he served approximately four months in federal custody.

Zhao completed his sentence before receiving the pardon. The clemency therefore did not release him from an ongoing prison term.

V. Binance’s corporate obligations

Binance’s resolution was separate from Zhao’s individual conviction.

Binance agreed to:

criminal forfeiture;
a criminal fine;
FinCEN and OFAC penalties;
compliance remediation;
transaction monitoring;
sanctions controls;
cooperation;
and independent monitoring.

This distinction is critical:

A pardon granted to Zhao did not, by itself, pardon Binance, vacate Binance’s corporate guilty plea, reimburse its penalties, terminate its monitorship, or excuse future violations.

Any reduction or termination of Binance’s separate obligations would require independent legal action, contractual modification, judicial approval, or agency action.

Investigators should determine whether such relief was later requested or provided.

VI. Binance’s relationship with World Liberty Financial

Following Trump’s return to office, Binance developed a commercially important relationship with World Liberty Financial and USD1.

Reported or documented forms of support included:

deployment of USD1 on BNB Smart Chain;
assistance allegedly provided in developing or promoting USD1;
participation in the MGX transaction settled using USD1;
listing USD1 for trading;
creation of multiple USD1 trading pairs;
zero-fee promotions;
support for USD1 liquidity;
later acceptance of USD1 within collateral arrangements; and
conversion of certain Binance-pegged BUSD collateral assets into USD1.

Binance’s own December 2025 announcement established new BNB/USD1, BTC/USD1, ETH/USD1, and SOL/USD1 trading pairs, fee promotions, and conversion of B-Token collateral from BUSD to USD1. Binance announcement.

These measures increased USD1’s utility and integration into the world’s largest cryptocurrency exchange.

VII. Alleged technical assistance

Bloomberg reporting, subsequently summarized by other publications, alleged that Binance assisted with code, promotion, and implementation connected to USD1.

Zhao disputed the reporting and characterized it as false. The allegation therefore remains contested.

It should not be stated as an established fact without:

source-code repositories;
developer communications;
contract records;
smart-contract deployment records;
audit files;
technical invoices;
or testimony from involved engineers.

If Binance personnel helped create USD1 while Zhao was seeking clemency, that would increase the significance of the relationship. It still would not prove a pardon-for-assistance agreement.

VIII. The MGX transaction

MGX, the Abu Dhabi state-backed investment company, invested approximately $2 billion in Binance for a minority interest.

World Liberty Financial’s USD1 stablecoin was selected to settle the transaction.

This produced several potential benefits:
EntityPotential benefitBinance$2 billion foreign institutional investmentMGXMinority interest in the world’s largest crypto exchangeWorld LibertyUSD1 adoption, reserves, potential income, liquidity and legitimacyBitGoIssuance, custody and reserve-management activityBNB Smart ChainTransaction activity and institutional validationTrump-affiliated interestsPotential indirect revenue and increased enterprise valueZhaoIncreased value and strength of a company in which he retained a substantial ownership interest
The precise value received by each party requires contracts and financial records.
IX. USD1 reserve-income implications
Approximately $2 billion of USD1 reportedly entered circulation shortly before the MGX closing announcement.
If reserve assets remained outstanding and generated interest, the gross yield could amount to tens of millions of dollars annually. The allocation of that income among World Liberty, BitGo, Binance, and other parties is not public in sufficient detail.
Later reserve reports showed sufficient assets backing reported redeemable USD1 on specified measurement dates. That evidence weighs against an unsupported allegation that the tokens were categorically unbacked.
The later reports do not disclose:
the transaction-specific source of the $2 billion;
continuous backing during every prior period;
the division of interest income;
special terms offered to MGX or Binance;
or side agreements.
X. SEC case against Binance
In February 2025, the SEC and Binance jointly sought a stay of the SEC’s civil enforcement case.
The requested stay followed:
Trump’s inauguration;
creation of the SEC Crypto Task Force;
the administration’s announced digital-asset-policy reversal; and

anticipation of a new regulatory framework.

Additional stays and eventual resolution or dismissal followed.

The SEC matter was civil and legally distinct from:

Zhao’s criminal conviction;

Binance’s DOJ guilty plea;

Treasury’s FinCEN and OFAC resolutions;

and the presidential pardon.
Investigators must not treat these separate proceedings as though the pardon automatically terminated all of them.
XI. Zhao’s pardon request
Zhao publicly confirmed in May 2025 that he had applied for a presidential pardon.
Before and during this period:

USD1 had been deployed on BNB Smart Chain;

the MGX transaction had been structured through USD1;

Zhao met with World Liberty participants in Abu Dhabi;

Binance’s relationship with USD1 was expanding;
and World Liberty’s commercial value was increasing.
These facts establish motive and opportunity for investigation, but not an agreement.
XII. Congressional inquiry

On May 14, 2025, Senators Elizabeth Warren, Richard Durbin, and Richard Blumenthal requested information from DOJ and the White House concerning:

Zhao’s pardon request;

White House and DOJ contacts with Zhao;
communications with Binance;
reported Binance–Trump-family business negotiations;
the SEC litigation stay;

World Liberty’s USD1 stablecoin;

and the MGX transaction.

The senators specifically asked whether Zhao’s pardon materials referenced Binance’s financial relationships with Trump-affiliated interests. Senate inquiry letter.

The letter establishes contemporaneous notice of the conflict concerns. Its contents do not independently prove the allegations.

XIII. The pardon
The formal clemency document states that Trump granted Zhao a “full and unconditional pardon” for the offense in United States v. Zhao, No. 23-CR-00179-RAJ-001.
The document is dated October 21, 2025. The pardon was publicly announced on October 23. Official DOJ clemency grant.
The White House characterized Zhao’s prosecution as part of the Biden administration’s “war on cryptocurrency.”
Trump subsequently said that he did not know Zhao personally and had been advised that Zhao had been treated unfairly.

Those statements should be compared with:

the pardon application;

recommendations supplied to Trump;

White House counsel records;
DOJ and Pardon Attorney memoranda;
lobbying contacts;
and communications from cryptocurrency-industry supporters.
XIV. Legal effect of the pardon
The pardon forgave Zhao’s federal offense and removed or mitigated many continuing legal consequences associated with that conviction.
It did not necessarily:

declare the admitted conduct factually nonexistent;

erase the historical court record;

pardon Binance;

return the criminal fine automatically;

terminate Binance’s compliance obligations;

invalidate Treasury’s sanctions findings;

dismiss unrelated civil cases;

authorize future violations;

or compel regulators to license Zhao or Binance.
The practical commercial effect could nevertheless be substantial.
A pardon may improve:
reputation;
licensing prospects;
ability to serve in management;

ability to conduct United States business;

investor confidence;

negotiation leverage;

and prospects for Binance’s reentry into regulated markets.
These potential benefits require jurisdiction-specific analysis.
XV. Binance support following the pardon
Binance’s deeper USD1 integration after the pardon is particularly relevant.
In December 2025, Binance announced:
additional USD1 trading pairs;
trading-bot support;
zero-fee USD1 promotions;
preferential liquidity arrangements; and

conversion of Binance-pegged BUSD collateral into USD1.

The timing permits at least three interpretations:

Binance expanded USD1 because it was commercially attractive.
Binance strengthened its relationship with World Liberty after receiving favorable United States treatment.
Commercial support and clemency were components of an agreed exchange.
Only the third interpretation supports the alleged corrupt-exchange theory. The present public record does not conclusively establish it.
XVI. Alleged exchange structure
flowchart TD
    A["Binance infrastructure and liquidity"] --> B["USD1 adoption and commercial value"]
    B --> C["Potential benefit to World Liberty interests"]
    D["Zhao clemency request"] --> E["Presidential pardon"]
    C -. "Alleged exchange requiring proof" .-> E
    E --> F["Potential reputational and regulatory benefit"]
The dashed relationship is the unproven element. It requires communications or other evidence establishing corrupt linkage.

XVII. Benefit-to-benefit chronology

DateEventPrincipal potential benefitNovember 2023Binance and Zhao plead guiltyFederal enforcement and compliance remediationJanuary 2025Trump begins pro-crypto administrationIndustry-wide policy benefitFebruary 2025SEC–Binance case stayedBinance and ZhaoMarch 2025USD1 launched on BNB Smart ChainWorld Liberty and Binance ecosystemApril 2025Approximately $2 billion USD1 issuedWorld Liberty’s liquidity and reserve baseMay 1, 2025MGX deal announced using USD1Binance, MGX and World LibertyMay 2025Zhao confirms pardon requestZhaoJuly 2025Report alleges Binance technical supportPotential World Liberty benefit; allegation disputedOctober 21, 2025Formal pardon grantedZhaoOctober 23, 2025Pardon publicly announcedZhao and potentially BinanceDecember 2025Binance materially expands USD1 integrationWorld Liberty and Binance ecosystem

The sequence supports investigation of a potential exchange. It does not establish one.

XVIII. Federal bribery analysis

A presidential pardon is an exercise of official power capable of constituting the official-action component of a bribery theory.
The remaining elements would require proof that:
Zhao, Binance, World Liberty, or an intermediary gave or promised something of value;
the value was intended to influence the pardon;
Trump or an agent understood the exchange;

and the pardon was granted pursuant to that corrupt understanding.

Potential “things of value” could include:
USD1 technical assistance;
selection of USD1 for the MGX transaction;

reserve-income generation;

exchange listings;

liquidity support;
favorable trading treatment;
investment in Trump-affiliated enterprises;
token purchases;
or other financial arrangements.
Commercial benefit plus a pardon is insufficient without corrupt linkage.
XIX. Gratuities analysis
A gratuities theory differs from bribery because it may concern value provided for an official act without proof that the exchange was agreed upon beforehand.

Application to the President, the precise statutory language, timing, and Supreme Court limitations would require careful legal analysis.

Evidence that Binance expanded USD1 specifically to reward the pardon would be materially different from evidence that Binance expanded it for independent commercial reasons.
The December 2025 integration should therefore be examined for both pre-pardon planning and post-pardon intent.
XX. Honest-services fraud
An honest-services theory generally requires a bribery or kickback scheme.
It cannot rest solely on:
conflicts of interest;
concealed access;
favorable policy;
a controversial pardon;
or benefits received by politically connected businesses.
The prosecution would need to prove the underlying corrupt exchange and the use of mails or wires in furtherance of it.
XXI. Wire-fraud theory
A wire-fraud theory might arise if participants used electronic communications to conceal a property-directed scheme involving:
sham transactions;
false statements about the reason for selecting USD1;
concealed revenue distributions;
disguised foreign payments;
false compliance representations;
or manipulation of the stablecoin’s reserve or supply.
The pardon itself is not property. The alleged scheme must be directed toward obtaining money or property and satisfy current federal fraud precedent.
XXII. Money laundering
The MGX transaction’s use of USD1 is not inherently money laundering.
A money-laundering theory would require proof that participants knowingly dealt with proceeds of specified unlawful activity or conducted transactions designed to:
conceal ownership;
conceal the source or control of funds;
promote underlying criminal activity;
avoid reporting requirements;
or evade sanctions.
No such conclusion can be drawn solely because the transaction used a stablecoin or Binance.
XXIII. Campaign-finance and foreign-influence questions
The Binance relationship could also raise campaign-finance or foreign-influence issues if commercial payments functioned as disguised political support.
Investigators should determine:
whether any token purchases were actually intended to influence Trump;
whether foreign nationals directed value through nominees;
whether World Liberty provided genuine market consideration;
whether preferential terms existed;
whether Binance or MGX discussed United States policy;
and whether transaction participants acted as agents of foreign principals.
An ordinary purchase or commercial transaction does not become a campaign contribution merely because the seller is associated with a politician.
XXIV. Clemency Clause and constitutional limits

The President’s clemency authority under Article II is exceptionally broad for federal offenses.

Courts generally provide limited review of a pardon’s wisdom or motive. That does not necessarily immunize separate criminal conduct used to procure a pardon.

For example, the constitutional pardon power would not necessarily protect:

bribery used to obtain the pardon;

false statements during the pardon process;
obstruction;
money laundering;
or conspiracy by private parties.

The investigation must distinguish the validity of the pardon from potentially unlawful conduct surrounding its procurement.

XXV. DOGE relevance
No public evidence presently establishes that DOGE formally participated in Zhao’s pardon.
The matter remains relevant to the DOGE investigation because the broader alleged structure includes:
executive centralization;
regulatory retrenchment;
elimination of enforcement capacity;
Trump-family commercial interests;
DOGE-adjacent technology networks;
foreign digital-asset capital;

and selective relief for politically connected technology executives.

Records should determine whether DOGE personnel:

communicated about Binance or Zhao;

recommended enforcement reductions affecting Binance;

accessed Binance-related agency data;

influenced staffing or budgets of relevant units;

or coordinated with Sacks, Hines, DOJ, Treasury, or the White House.

XXVI. RICO analysis

The pardon and surrounding transactions may constitute evidence of:

the alleged enterprise’s relationships;
a method for converting private support into official relief;
continuity between commercial and governmental participants;
motive;
benefit;

concealment;

and institutional control.

The pardon itself is not a statutory RICO predicate.

Potential predicates would require proof of independently chargeable:
bribery;
wire fraud;
money laundering;
obstruction;
unlawful foreign contributions;

sanctions violations;

or securities fraud.

A valid RICO analysis must identify who committed each predicate, when it occurred, how it furthered the enterprise, and what qualifying injury it caused.

XXVII. Evidence required

Investigators should obtain:
Zhao’s complete pardon application.
DOJ Office of the Pardon Attorney file.
White House counsel memoranda.
All recommendations presented to Trump.

Communications between Trump and pardon advisers.

Communications between Zhao, Binance, and pardon lobbyists.

