XXVIII. Counterevidence and exculpatory considerations
A balanced investigation must recognize:
Zhao admitted only a Bank Secrecy Act compliance offense and was not personally convicted of fraud.
Zhao completed his custodial sentence.
Trump publicly promised to reverse aggressive cryptocurrency enforcement.

The White House said the pardon ended an unjust “war on cryptocurrency.”

The President possesses broad constitutional clemency authority.
Binance and Zhao deny a commercial-for-pardon exchange.
Binance has independent commercial reasons to support a rapidly growing stablecoin.
USD1 was reportedly fully backed on later attestation dates.
The MGX investment benefited Binance directly.

World Liberty did not receive the $2 billion equity investment.

Binance’s corporate plea and compliance obligations were not automatically pardoned.

The SEC’s civil enforcement decisions were legally separate from clemency.
No publicly available communication presently proves a quid pro quo.
XXIX. Preliminary investigative finding
Established: Binance admitted serious, systematic AML, money-transmission, and sanctions violations and paid more than $4 billion in associated resolutions.
Established: Zhao pleaded guilty, paid a fine, resigned as CEO, and served a federal sentence.
Established: After Trump returned to office, Binance supported or integrated USD1 through BNB Smart Chain deployment, the MGX settlement, trading pairs, liquidity incentives, and later collateral use.
Established: World Liberty and Trump-affiliated interests potentially received substantial financial and commercial benefits from Binance’s adoption of USD1.

Established: Zhao sought a presidential pardon during this developing commercial relationship, and Trump granted a full and unconditional pardon dated October 21, 2025.

Established: Binance expanded USD1’s platform role after the pardon.

Reasonable inference: Binance, Zhao, and World Liberty possessed potentially reciprocal interests: Binance could confer substantial commercial value on a Trump-affiliated enterprise, while the President could confer clemency and influence the regulatory environment affecting Zhao and Binance.
Not established: That Binance’s USD1 support was consideration for the pardon; that Trump agreed to a corrupt exchange; that Zhao personally directed the MGX transaction to obtain clemency; or that DOGE participated in the pardon decision.
RICO significance: The matter presents a substantial corruption and enterprise inquiry because it combines a presidential pardon, admitted financial-compliance violations, foreign state-connected capital, and financial benefits to a presidentially affiliated company. A criminal allegation requires the missing communications, contracts, revenue records, and pardon file.
