XX. Potential conversion mechanism

The alleged mechanism can be summarized as follows:

flowchart TD
    A["Campaign, PAC and inaugural support"] --> B["White House access"]
    B --> C["Policy requests and private consultation"]
    C --> D["Enforcement, reserve and legislative action"]
    D --> E["Higher legitimacy and commercial opportunity"]
    E --> F["Value to donors and affiliated ventures"]
This is an investigative hypothesis. Each arrow requires evidence. Temporal sequence and shared benefit cannot substitute for proof of agreement, intent, and causation.
XXI. Federal bribery analysis
Under 18 U.S.C. § 201, an actionable bribery theory would require proof of a corrupt offer, demand, receipt, or agreement involving something of value in exchange for an official act.
A viable theory might arise if evidence showed:
a donation was requested in exchange for summit admission;
an executive promised campaign support in exchange for dismissal of a case;

token purchases were solicited in exchange for access or regulatory treatment;

an official directed an agency action after accepting a connected payment;

or a foreign purchaser acquired Trump-affiliated tokens pursuant to an agreement for governmental action.
The public record presently shows contribution, access, and benefit. It does not conclusively show the necessary exchange.
XXII. Campaign and inaugural-finance analysis
Investigators should distinguish:
campaign contributions;
super-PAC independent expenditures;
inaugural donations;
political-party contributions;
lobbying payments;
payments to Trump-affiliated businesses;
token purchases;
transaction fees; and
foreign transfers.
These categories have different legal rules.
A token purchase from a presidentially affiliated venture is particularly difficult to evaluate because it may economically benefit the seller without being reported as a political contribution.
If made for the purpose of influencing an election or official action rather than acquiring an asset on ordinary commercial terms, the transaction could warrant campaign-finance, bribery, emoluments, or money-laundering analysis depending on the purchaser and facts.
XXIII. Foreign-influence and emoluments questions
Digital-asset purchases may occur through pseudonymous wallets, offshore exchanges, nominees, market makers, and decentralized protocols.
This creates a risk that:
foreign governments;
sovereign wealth funds;
foreign nationals;
prohibited contributors;
sanctioned actors;
or intermediaries acting for them
could transfer value to a president-affiliated venture without ordinary political-disclosure mechanisms.
An Emoluments Clause or foreign-bribery analysis would require identifying the beneficial purchaser and determining whether the value came from a foreign state or instrumentality.
Anonymous ownership is a reason to investigate, not proof of foreign-government involvement.
XXIV. Wire fraud, securities fraud, and market manipulation
Potential securities or commodities theories depend on the legal status of each asset and the conduct involved.
Relevant evidence could include:
false statements about government acquisition plans;
advance disclosure of reserve announcements;
coordinated trading before presidential posts;
wash trading or artificial volume;
undisclosed promotional compensation;
manipulation of token supply;
misleading claims about presidential involvement;
or concealment of the economic interests retained by affiliated entities.
An official announcement that moves a market is not manipulation by itself. Proof of deceptive conduct, intent, jurisdiction, and trading benefit is required.
XXV. RICO analysis

The summit could serve as evidence of:

association among alleged enterprise participants;

access relationships;
shared objectives;
opportunity to coordinate;
continuity between campaign financing and government action;
methods of distributing regulatory benefits;
and the intersection of public authority with private ventures.
The summit itself is not a RICO predicate.
A RICO theory would require predicate offenses such as provable:

bribery;

wire fraud;

money laundering;

obstruction;

unlawful foreign payments;
or qualifying securities fraud.
The evidence must also establish a pattern, an enterprise distinct from the alleged violations where required, participation in the enterprise’s affairs, and legally cognizable injury.
XXVI. Evidence required
Investigators should obtain:

The complete summit invitation and attendance lists.

Selection criteria and declined invitations.

White House and Secret Service visitor logs.

Seating charts and photographs.
Video and audio of closed portions of the summit.
Meeting minutes, notes, presentations, and briefing materials.
Documents submitted by each private participant.
Communications between participants and Sacks, Hines, Trump, Bessent, and Lutnick.
Inaugural-committee contribution records.
Fairshake and affiliated PAC expenditure records.
America PAC communications involving crypto policy.
Token-wallet records used for Ripple’s inaugural donation.

Beneficial-ownership information for contributing entities.

Communications concerning Coinbase and Kraken enforcement cases.

SEC contacts with the White House concerning dismissals.

Communications involving Zach Witkoff and World Liberty Financial.

World Liberty Financial ownership and revenue agreements.

$TRUMP token ownership, fee, and market-maker records.

Trading records surrounding reserve announcements.
Records identifying persons with advance knowledge.
Foreign and sovereign purchases of Trump-affiliated assets.
Ethics reviews concerning the President’s crypto businesses.
Drafts of the summit remarks and policy announcements.
Communications concerning stablecoin and market-structure legislation.

Evidence concerning custody and payment-system contracts.

XXVII. Counterevidence and exculpatory considerations

A balanced investigation must recognize:

Industry summits are common forms of executive consultation.

Cryptocurrency companies possess relevant technical expertise.

Attendance does not establish purchase of access.

Inaugural contributions may legally come from corporations.
The administration publicly announced its crypto policies during the campaign.
Congress maintained independent authority and bipartisan interest in digital-asset legislation.
The Strategic Bitcoin Reserve initially relied on forfeited assets.
The summit did not produce an immediate open-market bitcoin-purchasing program.
Some market participants were disappointed by the summit’s limited immediate results.
Sacks’s reported divestitures constitute material conflict-mitigation evidence.
Agency enforcement reversals may reflect changed legal interpretations rather than corruption.

World Liberty Financial’s presence creates an appearance issue but does not prove its representative dictated policy.

No publicly established communication yet demonstrates a donation-for-policy exchange.

DOGE-30 — Federal Stablecoin Legislation, Treasury-Demand Policy, Private-Issuer Licensing, and the Alleged Replacement of Public Money with Corporate Dollar Tokens

I. Investigative purpose

This section examines whether federal digital-asset policy deliberately selected privately issued payment stablecoins as the preferred form of digital-dollar infrastructure while terminating development of a publicly issued central-bank digital currency.

The central factual sequence is:

The administration prohibited federal agencies from developing or promoting a central-bank digital currency;
It simultaneously directed the government to promote privately issued dollar-backed stablecoins;
Congress enacted the GENIUS Act, creating federal and state licensing pathways for private issuers;
Stablecoin issuers were permitted to earn income from reserves composed substantially of dollars, Treasury obligations, and Treasury-secured instruments;
Issuers and distribution partners could retain that income while holders were prohibited from receiving issuer-paid yield merely for holding the token; and
Private companies consequently received a federal pathway to provide digital-dollar services that the federal government had prohibited itself from developing.

The evidence supports an investigation into the delegation and privatization of digital monetary infrastructure. It does not establish that Federal Reserve notes, bank deposits, or public money have already been legally replaced.

II. The federal policy substitution

On January 23, 2025, President Donald J. Trump issued an executive order titled Strengthening American Leadership in Digital Financial Technology.

The order announced two complementary policies:

Promoting the development and worldwide growth of lawful dollar-backed stablecoins; and

Prohibiting the establishment, issuance, circulation, or use of a federal CBDC within the United States.
The order directed agencies to terminate ongoing CBDC initiatives while establishing a President’s Working Group on Digital Asset Markets to develop a federal framework for stablecoins and other digital assets. January 23, 2025 executive order

This was not merely a decision to leave digital-money development to the market. It was an affirmative policy choice between two models:

Public modelPrivate modelCentral-bank digital currencyCorporate payment stablecoinDirect central-bank liabilityPrivate issuer’s redemption obligationPublic issuanceLicensed private issuancePublicly controlled ledger or platformCorporate smart contracts and private platformsGovernment retains monetary economicsPrivate firms retain reserve economicsPublic-law governanceStatutory regulation plus private contractsDirect government accountabilityRegulator-supervised corporate administration
The federal government prohibited one model while promoting the other.
III. What the executive order did—and did not do
The executive order defined a CBDC as digital money denominated in the national unit of account that constitutes a direct liability of the central bank.
It prohibited agencies, except where required by law, from:
Establishing a CBDC;
Issuing a CBDC;
Promoting a CBDC;
Circulating a CBDC;
Using a CBDC; or
Continuing existing federal CBDC development initiatives.
At the same time, it declared that dollar-backed stablecoins could promote the sovereignty of the United States dollar.
The order did not itself:
Authorize private companies to issue legal tender;
Give stablecoins Federal Reserve backing;
Require government agencies to accept USDC or other stablecoins;
Replace physical currency;
Eliminate commercial-bank deposits;
Appropriate money for stablecoin adoption; or

Grant Circle, Coinbase, or another identified firm an exclusive federal franchise.

Those distinctions must be preserved.

IV. Enactment of the GENIUS Act

On July 18, 2025, President Trump signed the GENIUS Act into law as Public Law 119-27.

The Act created the first comprehensive federal statutory framework specifically governing payment stablecoins. It defined a payment stablecoin as a digital asset designed for payment or settlement whose issuer promises redemption or repurchase for a fixed amount of monetary value and represents that the asset will maintain a stable value. GENIUS Act, Public Law 119-27

The statute expressly distinguishes payment stablecoins from national currency. A stablecoin is therefore not transformed into a Federal Reserve note or government currency merely because it is regulated under federal law.
V. Private-issuer licensing
The GENIUS Act permits several categories of entities to become authorized stablecoin issuers, including:
Approved nonbank entities;
Approved uninsured national banks;
Approved federal branches;

Subsidiaries of insured depository institutions;

Credit-union-related entities; and

Qualifying state-supervised issuers.
A nonbank company may therefore receive federal approval to issue a token functioning as a digital dollar substitute without becoming an insured commercial bank.
This represents a major delegation of payment infrastructure. The private issuer receives authority to:
Mint the token;
Redeem it;
Select supported networks;
Establish customer-access arrangements;

Contract with distributors;

administer reserve assets;

Exercise technical controls; and

Earn income connected to circulation.

Federal and state regulators supervise the issuer, but the government does not ordinarily operate the token.

VI. One-to-one reserve requirement

The GENIUS Act requires permitted issuers to maintain identifiable reserves backing outstanding payment stablecoins on at least a one-to-one basis.

Permitted reserve assets include specified categories of highly liquid assets, such as:

U.S. dollars;

Deposits at insured depository institutions;
Short-term Treasury obligations;
Certain repurchase agreements secured by Treasury instruments;
Certain reverse-repurchase arrangements;
Government money-market funds invested in permitted assets; and
Other narrowly authorized liquid instruments.

This requirement reduces the risk of purely algorithmic or uncollateralized issuance.

However, one-to-one reserve regulation does not make a stablecoin identical to public money. A holder remains dependent on:

The issuer’s accurate accounting;
Reserve custody;
Banking access;
Redemption operations;
Technology;
Regulatory supervision; and
Insolvency procedures.
VII. No transaction-level proof requirement

The GENIUS Act requires monthly reserve disclosure and examination of the previous month-end information by a registered public accounting firm. It also requires monthly certifications by the issuer’s chief executive and chief financial officers, with criminal consequences for knowingly false certifications.

Those are meaningful protections.

But the Act does not clearly require the public to receive:

Real-time reserve reporting;

Transaction-by-transaction deposit proof;

Cryptographic linkage between a fiat deposit and each mint;
Continuous intraday liability reconciliation;
Public disclosure of authorized-participant credit;
Bank settlement-finality timestamps;
Administrative-key logs; or
Proof that every mint was preceded by finally settled fiat funds.
The blockchain proves that tokens were minted. It does not independently prove that an equivalent deposit settled beforehand.

Accordingly, licensed issuers may remain capable of creating tokens through centralized administrative authority while the public receives aggregate, periodic assurance afterward.

That is not proof that permitted issuers are minting unbacked tokens. It is a continuing transparency gap that regulators must address through rulemaking and examination.

VIII. Prohibition on issuer-paid interest to holders
The GENIUS Act provides that a permitted issuer or foreign payment-stablecoin issuer may not pay a holder interest or yield solely for holding, using, or retaining the stablecoin.
This provision creates a significant economic allocation:
Holders provide the dollars supporting issuance.
Issuers invest those dollars in qualifying reserve assets.
Reserve assets generate interest.
The issuer cannot simply pass interest to the holder as issuer-paid yield for holding the stablecoin.

Reserve-derived economics may instead remain with issuers, distributors, reserve managers, custodians, or other contractual participants.

In the case of USDC, Circle has disclosed substantial reserve income and substantial distribution payments to Coinbase.

The statute’s prohibition does not necessarily prevent every third-party reward program, marketing payment, or exchange incentive. Its exact application depends on implementing regulations and the structure of the arrangement.

Investigators should determine whether the restriction:

Protects stablecoins from being treated like deposits or securities;

Prevents destabilizing competition for yield;
Preserves profits for issuers and distributors;
Shifts rewards into less transparent affiliate arrangements; or
Creates opportunities for regulatory arbitrage.
IX. Public money versus corporate token
A user may experience a stablecoin as a digital dollar, but the legal claims are different:
FeatureFederal Reserve noteBank depositPayment stablecoinIssuerFederal Reserve systemCommercial bankPermitted private issuerLegal tenderYes, under federal lawNo, but payable in dollarsNoDirect claimPublic currencyClaim against bankRedemption claim against issuerDeposit insuranceNot applicableGenerally available within limitsNot automaticallyReserve incomePublic/central-bank and banking systemPrimarily bank economicsIssuer and partner economicsAdministrative freezeThrough lawful governmental/banking processBank and government controlsIssuer smart-contract and legal controlsTechnical governancePublic monetary systemBank systemsPrivate smart contracts and platformsInsolvency exposureSovereign systemBank-resolution regimeSpecial stablecoin priority and reserve process
The alleged replacement is therefore functional rather than presently legal: corporate stablecoins may increasingly perform payment and settlement functions previously performed through cash, bank deposits, card networks, or potentially a public CBDC.

X. Misrepresentation protections confirm private status

The GENIUS Act expressly provides that payment stablecoins are not backed by the full faith and credit of the United States and are not guaranteed by the federal government.

It prohibits issuers from representing that a stablecoin:

Is legal tender;

Is issued by the United States;

Is guaranteed by the United States;

Is approved by the government in a manner that would mislead users; or

Is federally insured.
These protections confirm that Congress recognized a foreseeable risk: consumers may incorrectly treat a federally regulated corporate token as federally guaranteed money.
Federal licensing can create an appearance of public backing even where the statute expressly denies it.
XI. Reserve-income privatization
The stablecoin structure transfers valuable economics to private parties:

flowchart TD
    A["Users exchange dollars for stablecoins"] --> B["Private issuer holds reserves"]
    B --> C["Treasuries, deposits, and secured repos"]
    C --> D["Interest and dividend income"]
    D --> E["Issuer economics"]
    D --> F["Distributor economics"]
    D --> G["Manager and custodian fees"]
    H["Federal licensing and dollar credibility"] --> B

The government supplies:

The dollar denomination;

Treasury instruments;
Banking-system access;
Regulatory legitimacy;
Courts and enforcement;
Sanctions authority; and

A statutory licensing framework.

Private participants receive:

Reserve income;
Management fees;
Custody fees;
Distribution payments;
Customer relationships;
Transaction information;
Intellectual-property value;
Network control; and
Equity appreciation.
This structure is not inherently unlawful, but it is a recognizable privatization of digital-dollar infrastructure and its accompanying revenue.
XII. Treasury-demand policy
Stablecoin issuers must acquire reserve assets as circulation grows. When those assets include Treasury obligations and Treasury-secured repos, stablecoin expansion creates demand for federal debt instruments.
This produces alignment between:
Treasury’s interest in broad and stable demand for federal debt;
The administration’s interest in worldwide dollar usage;
Stablecoin issuers’ interest in increasing circulation;
Asset managers’ interest in increasing assets under management;
Custodians’ interest in increasing custody volumes; and
Distributors’ interest in reserve-linked payments and customer balances.
The relationship does not mean stablecoins cause federal debt or authorize deficit spending. But it can make private stablecoin expansion attractive to a government financing very large short-term borrowing requirements.
XIII. Potential policy circularity
The potential policy loop is:
Federal deficits require substantial Treasury issuance.
Stablecoin legislation creates additional private demand for Treasury-backed reserve assets.
Treasury yield produces issuer and distributor income.
Stablecoin firms use part of their increased economic capacity for lobbying, advocacy, and political spending.
Policymakers enact measures encouraging further stablecoin use.
Increased circulation produces additional reserve demand and private income.
This sequence is not criminal unless supported by evidence of bribery, fraud, unlawful coordination, or another predicate offense.
It nevertheless presents a regulatory-capture risk because the private firms benefiting from public-debt interest may finance the political process governing their own licensing and market expansion.
XIV. The public-CBDC prohibition as market allocation
The January 2025 executive order removed a potential government competitor from the digital-dollar market.
A public CBDC could have offered:
A direct central-bank liability;
No private issuer credit risk;
Publicly determined technical standards;
Direct monetary-policy integration;
Potentially lower intermediary costs; and
A publicly controlled alternative to corporate stablecoins.
A CBDC could also create legitimate concerns involving:
Government surveillance;

Financial privacy;

Cybersecurity;

Bank disintermediation;

Political control of payments;

Operational concentration; and

Direct government account restrictions.
The executive order resolved that policy contest in favor of prohibiting the public model and promoting the private model.
This supports investigation into whether the prohibition was adopted:
Solely for privacy and economic-liberty reasons;
To protect banks from public competition;

To benefit stablecoin issuers and exchanges;

In response to lobbying or political spending;

To expand private Treasury demand; or

Through some combination of legitimate and industry-aligned objectives.

XV. Stablecoins do not eliminate surveillance

The stated rejection of a CBDC rested partly on privacy and surveillance concerns. But replacing a public CBDC with private stablecoins does not automatically protect privacy.
Private stablecoin networks may enable:
Public blockchain transaction tracing;
Customer identification by exchanges;
Address clustering;
Smart-contract freezing;
Sanctions blocking;
Automated transaction monitoring;
Data sharing with law enforcement;
Commercial profiling; and

Cross-platform behavioral analysis.

The difference may be who initially holds and administers the information—not whether the information exists.
A privatized surveillance structure may be less visible because data is distributed across issuers, exchanges, analytics firms, banks, and custodians rather than held inside one government ledger.
XVI. Lawful-order and freezing authority
The GENIUS Act recognizes lawful orders requiring issuers to:
Seize stablecoins;
Freeze stablecoins;
Burn tokens; or

Prevent transfers.

This confirms that payment stablecoins are not necessarily censorship-resistant bearer cash. They are privately administered instruments subject to government orders and issuer-controlled technical functions.
Such powers may be necessary to address:
Theft;
Fraud;
Sanctions;
Terrorist financing;
Money laundering;
Court judgments; and
Asset forfeiture.

But due-process protections must address:

Mistaken address attribution;

Inadvertent receipt of tainted tokens;

Indefinite freezing;

Lack of notice;

Delayed judicial review;
Cross-border conflicts;
Private blacklists;
Algorithmic error; and
Government reliance on unverified vendor intelligence.
XVII. Bank Secrecy Act coverage

The GENIUS Act treats permitted payment-stablecoin issuers as financial institutions for Bank Secrecy Act purposes and subjects them to applicable anti-money-laundering and sanctions requirements.

That is an important safeguard.

However, the Act’s coverage must be tested across the entire transaction chain:

Issuer;

Exchange;
Custodian;
Wallet provider;
Blockchain front end;
Liquidity pool;
Bridge;

Validator;

Foreign distributor;

Decentralized protocol;

Market maker; and

Beneficial owner.

If the issuer is regulated but major transaction pathways remain outside equivalent controls, illicit activity may migrate away from the licensed redemption perimeter while continuing to use the token.

XVIII. State and federal licensing fragmentation
The Act permits both federal and qualifying state supervisory pathways.
This can preserve state expertise and regulatory competition. It can also create:
Regulatory arbitrage;
Differing examination standards;
Inconsistent consumer protection;

Competition among states for issuer registrations;

Difficulties supervising multistate activity;
Federal–state conflicts;
Uneven capital requirements; and
Opportunities for large issuers to influence the most favorable jurisdiction.
The Act imposes additional federal oversight mechanisms around large state-qualified issuers, including a $10 billion consolidated issuance threshold, subject to statutory procedures and potential waivers.
Investigators should determine whether waivers, equivalence findings, or supervisory transfers are granted consistently and without political influence.
XIX. Insolvency protections and remaining risk

The Act gives stablecoin holders priority claims against required reserve assets in an issuer insolvency. It also creates procedures intended to facilitate redemption from available reserves.

This is materially stronger than leaving holders as ordinary unsecured creditors.

But statutory priority does not guarantee full and immediate payment if:
Reserves are deficient;
Ownership records are inaccurate;
Assets are inaccessible;
Custodians fail;
Liens or competing claims exist;

Tokens were minted before deposits settled;

Fraudulent mints occurred;

Reserve instruments decline in value;

Multiple blockchains report inconsistent supply; or

Litigation delays distribution.
Priority determines who receives available assets first. It does not create assets that are missing.
XX. Government adoption and functional replacement
Corporate stablecoins would move closer to replacing public money if federal agencies began using them for:
Tax collections;
Tax refunds;
Social Security;
Medicare or Medicaid payments;
Veterans’ benefits;

Disaster relief;

Federal payroll;

Contractor payments;

Foreign assistance;

Customs settlement;

Treasury securities settlement; or
International government transfers.
Such use could make a private issuer essential to the delivery of public benefits.
The investigation must identify whether any agency has:

Conducted a USDC or stablecoin pilot;

Requested industry proposals;

Signed a memorandum of understanding;
Developed government wallet infrastructure;
Shared payment-system data;
Modified procurement rules;
Considered stablecoins in payment-modernization plans; or
Consulted DOGE personnel about private-token adoption.
Federal recognition alone is not proof of operational adoption.

XXI. DOGE nexus

The DOGE-related inquiry should determine whether DOGE personnel or associated private advisers:
Recommended replacing legacy federal payment systems with corporate stablecoins;
Advocated private tokens while opposing a public CBDC;
Communicated with Circle, Coinbase, BlackRock, BNY, Paxos, PayPal, Ripple, Tether, or World Liberty Financial;
Supplied industry participants with information concerning federal disbursement systems;
Recommended personnel removals affecting stablecoin regulation;
Pressured Treasury, the OCC, FDIC, Federal Reserve, SEC, CFTC, or FinCEN;
Excluded cryptocurrency firms from government-efficiency reviews;
Coordinated government-payment modernization with political donors;

Drafted or transmitted legislative language; or

Sought government contracts involving stablecoin custody, settlement, or compliance.

No reviewed evidence presently proves such DOGE coordination.

XXII. Presidential and private-token conflicts

Any evaluation of stablecoin legislation must separately examine officials with financial interests in private digital-asset ventures.

The relevant questions include:
Did an official or family member possess an interest in a stablecoin issuer?
Did legislation increase the value or utility of that stablecoin?
Were ethics officials consulted?
Were recusals requested or issued?
Did foreign persons purchase or use the affiliated token?
Did government officials promote general stablecoin adoption while omitting personal financial interests?
Did an affiliated issuer receive access unavailable to competitors?
Were official acts connected to token investments, political contributions, or foreign capital?
These issues are addressed more directly in DOGE-14 concerning World Liberty Financial and USD1. They should be incorporated by reference rather than conflated with USDC without supporting evidence.
XXIII. Political financing and legislative benefit
Coinbase and other digital-asset companies openly supported large political-spending and advocacy programs before and after the 2024 election.

The GENIUS Act delivered a valuable statutory framework sought by much of the industry.

This establishes:

Political spending;

Policy advocacy;

Subsequent legislative action; and

A substantial industry benefit.
It does not establish a corrupt quid pro quo.
To prove bribery, honest-services fraud, or a RICO predicate, investigators would need evidence connecting a contribution or other thing of value to an agreed official act, such as:
A licensing decision;
A statutory carveout;
A regulatory exemption;
Suppression of an enforcement matter;
Approval of a particular issuer;

Exclusion of a competitor; or

Government adoption of a specific token.

XXIV. Possible legal theories

Depending on the evidence, potential violations could include:

Federal bribery;

Federal-program bribery;

Honest-services fraud involving bribery or kickbacks;

Mail or wire fraud;
False statements in licensing applications;
False reserve certifications;
Bank fraud;
Securities or commodities fraud;
Procurement fraud;
Campaign-finance violations;
Undisclosed conflicts of interest;
Money laundering;
Sanctions violations;

Obstruction;

Antitrust violations; or

Unauthorized misuse of government-payment information.

A RICO theory would require a qualifying enterprise, knowing participation, at least two related predicate acts, continuity, and legally cognizable injury.

Licensing private stablecoins, holding Treasury reserves, receiving reserve income, lobbying, and making political contributions are not independently racketeering acts.
XXV. Evidence required
Investigators should obtain:
Executive-branch CBDC termination records;
Federal Reserve CBDC research closed after January 2025;
President’s Working Group meeting records;
Working Group participant and visitor lists;
Drafts of the GENIUS Act;
Legislative metadata;
Industry-proposed language;
Circle, Coinbase, and stablecoin-association communications;
Treasury and OCC rulemaking records;

Issuer-licensing applications;

State-equivalence determinations;

$10 billion-threshold waiver records;
Reserve-certification procedures;
Accounting-firm workpapers;
Deposit-to-mint reconciliations;
Administrative-key controls;
Lawful freeze and burn orders;
Government stablecoin pilot records;
DOGE payment-modernization communications;

Political-contribution records;

Lobbyist contacts and calendars;

Ethics disclosures and waivers;

Treasury-demand projections;

Stablecoin-run stress tests;
Resolution and emergency-liquidity plans; and
Communications concerning possible federal stabilization.

DOGE-24 — DOJ Cryptocurrency Enforcement Retrenchment, NCET Dissolution, Platform-Liability Restrictions, and the Alleged Deactivation of Federal Illicit-Finance Investigations

I. Scope and investigative purpose

This exhibit examines the Department of Justice’s April 7, 2025 reversal of federal cryptocurrency-enforcement policy, including:

Immediate dissolution of the National Cryptocurrency Enforcement Team;
Termination of cryptocurrency work by the Market Integrity and Major Frauds Unit;
Restrictions on cases involving exchanges, mixers, wallets, and infrastructure providers;
Increased approval requirements for certain charging theories;
Closure of investigations deemed inconsistent with the new policy;
Transfer of responsibility to ordinary U.S. Attorney’s Offices;
DOJ participation in the President’s Working Group on Digital Asset Markets; and
The potential involvement of DOGE, the White House, political donors, and private cryptocurrency interests.

The central question is whether DOJ implemented a lawful enforcement-priority change or deliberately weakened institutional capacity to investigate politically connected cryptocurrency platforms and illicit-finance networks.

II. Creation and original mission of NCET

The Department of Justice created the National Cryptocurrency Enforcement Team in October 2021.

NCET’s original purpose was to combine expertise from:

The Money Laundering and Asset Recovery Section;

The Computer Crime and Intellectual Property Section;
U.S. Attorney’s Offices;
Federal investigative agencies;
State, local, tribal, and territorial authorities; and
Foreign law-enforcement partners.
Its mandate included investigating and prosecuting:

Virtual-currency exchanges;

Mixing and tumbling services;
Money-laundering infrastructure;
Ransomware payments;
Darknet markets;
Narcotics trafficking;
Human trafficking;
Unregistered money-services businesses;
Professional money launderers;
Fraud and extortion;
Digital-asset seizure and forfeiture; and
Other criminal misuse of cryptocurrency.
DOJ announcement establishing NCET

NCET was therefore designed not merely to prosecute individual cryptocurrency users, but to examine financial intermediaries and infrastructure actors that knowingly enabled or profited from criminal activity.

III. NCET’s national coordination function

NCET performed functions that could not easily be reproduced by a single local prosecutor’s office:

Centralized blockchain and financial expertise;

Developed nationwide charging strategies;

Supported complex cross-district investigations;
Coordinated cryptocurrency seizures;
Connected prosecutors with specialists;
Maintained international relationships;
Trained federal, state, local, and tribal authorities;
Shared technical and evidentiary methods;
Developed search, seizure, restraint, and forfeiture practices; and
Addressed platforms operating across several jurisdictions.
Digital-asset investigations frequently cross:
Federal districts;

State boundaries;

National borders;
Multiple blockchains;
Exchanges and bridges;
Centralized and decentralized platforms;
Corporate and self-custodied wallets; and
Criminal, civil, regulatory, and sanctions jurisdiction.
Dissolving a centralized team may therefore have consequences beyond the number of prosecutors reassigned.
IV. 2023 expansion before dissolution

In July 2023, DOJ merged NCET into the Computer Crime and Intellectual Property Section while preserving its mission.

DOJ described NCET at that time as its leading concentration of cryptocurrency-knowledgeable criminal attorneys. It stated that the merger would:

More than double the attorneys potentially available for cryptocurrency cases;

Give NCET’s director charging authority;

Expand tracing and seizure capabilities;

Strengthen ransomware investigations; and
Increase support for prosecutors throughout the country.
DOJ identified NCET’s work in matters involving Bitzlato and manipulation of the Mango Markets decentralized exchange, as well as its role in establishing the Digital Asset Coordinator network. DOJ’s 2023 NCET expansion announcement
Less than two years later, DOJ terminated the specialized team.
V. April 7, 2025 DOJ memorandum

Deputy Attorney General Todd Blanche issued the memorandum Ending Regulation by Prosecution on April 7, 2025.

The memorandum declared that:

The digital-asset industry was important to national economic development;

DOJ was not a digital-asset regulator;

DOJ would no longer use criminal prosecutions to impose regulatory frameworks;

Enforcement would focus on persons committing fraud or using cryptocurrency for independently criminal purposes;

Platforms would generally not be pursued for users’ conduct or unwitting regulatory violations;
Inconsistent ongoing investigations should be closed;
Certain licensing, registration, and classification cases should not be charged without additional requirements;
The Market Integrity and Major Frauds Unit would cease cryptocurrency enforcement;
NCET would be disbanded immediately; and
DOJ would participate in the President’s Working Group on Digital Asset Markets.
DOJ’s April 7, 2025 directive
The directive is direct evidence of institutional retrenchment. Whether that retrenchment was corrupt depends upon its origin, implementation, selectivity, and effects.
VI. Conduct that remained an enforcement priority
The memorandum did not terminate every cryptocurrency prosecution.

It expressly preserved priority for cases involving:

Embezzlement;

Misappropriation of customer assets;

Investment fraud;

Cryptocurrency scams;

Fake projects and rug pulls;

Exchange and DAO hacking;
Smart-contract exploitation;
Cartels;
Transnational criminal organizations;
Terrorist organizations;
Narcotics and fentanyl trafficking;
Human trafficking and smuggling;
Organized crime;
Gang financing;
Sanctions evasion; and
Criminal activity by foreign adversaries.
This preserved authority is important exculpatory evidence against the broad claim that DOJ stopped enforcing cryptocurrency-related criminal law entirely.
The narrower concern is whether DOJ retained offenses in theory while removing the specialized structure and intermediary cases necessary to investigate them effectively.
VII. Platform-liability restriction
The memorandum stated that DOJ would not target exchanges, mixing services, and offline wallets for the acts of end users or for unwitting regulatory violations, except where consistent with identified enforcement priorities.
It further indicated that DOJ would pursue criminal organizations using digital assets but generally would not pursue the platforms those organizations used.

This distinction can protect legitimate platforms from strict liability for criminal acts they neither knew about nor intended.

It also presents a potential enforcement gap where a platform:

Knowingly services criminal customers;

Deliberately avoids customer identification;

Ignores repeated law-enforcement notices;

Disables effective transaction monitoring;

Markets itself to prohibited users;

Profits disproportionately from high-risk transactions;

Helps customers evade sanctions;
Conceals beneficial ownership;
Structures transactions to defeat reporting;
Falsifies compliance records;
Provides direct technical assistance; or
Maintains plausible deniability through deliberately fragmented operations.
The directive should not be interpreted as legal immunity for knowing participation, conspiracy, aiding and abetting, deliberate falsification, or laundering criminal proceeds.
VIII. Willfulness threshold for regulatory offenses
The memorandum directed prosecutors not to charge specified regulatory offenses unless evidence established that the defendant knew of the relevant licensing or registration obligation and violated it willfully.
The affected theories included:
Certain unlicensed money-transmission cases;
Bank Secrecy Act violations;

Unregistered securities offerings;

Unregistered broker-dealer violations; and

Commodity Exchange Act registration violations.

The memorandum acknowledged that this heightened threshold was an internal enforcement priority and was not necessarily required by the underlying statutes in every context.

This distinction matters. Executive enforcement policy may decline cases that Congress legally authorized, but it cannot amend the statutory elements permanently.

The investigation should determine whether the additional willfulness requirement was applied uniformly or selectively to politically connected entities.
IX. Restriction on security-versus-commodity litigation
The directive advised against charges requiring DOJ to litigate whether a digital asset is a security or commodity when an adequate alternative criminal charge exists.
It generally permitted DOJ to:
Treat bitcoin or ether as commodities for specified charging purposes;

Use mail or wire fraud instead of disputed regulatory classifications; and

Charge securities fraud where traditional equity or stock was the relevant security.

Exceptions required approval by the Deputy Attorney General or a designee.

Potential consequences include:

Fewer judicial decisions clarifying token status;

Higher approval barriers;

Reduced use of registration offenses;

Greater reliance on general fraud statutes;
Difficulty prosecuting misconduct before provable victim loss occurs; and
Unequal treatment between digital-asset and traditional financial businesses.

Conversely, the policy may avoid using criminal cases to resolve genuinely unsettled regulatory classifications.

X. Closure of ongoing investigations

The memorandum directed that investigations inconsistent with the new policy should be closed.
This instruction is one of the most consequential portions of the directive because it potentially affected already-developed matters involving:
Evidence collected under subpoena;
Grand-jury proceedings;
Cooperating witnesses;
Foreign requests;

Suspicious-activity reports;

Blockchain tracing;

Asset restraints;

Victim complaints;

Parallel civil investigations; and

Statutes of limitation.
The investigation should obtain a complete case-disposition inventory identifying:
Matter number;
Investigating component;
Principal subjects;
Alleged offenses;
Funds traced;
Political or commercial connections;
Status on April 7, 2025;
Staff recommendation;

Closure or transfer decision;

Decision-maker;
Evidence-preservation status; and
Statute-of-limitations consequences.
Aggregate statistics alone will not show whether politically connected investigations received preferential treatment.
XI. NCET’s immediate dissolution
The directive disbanded NCET “effective immediately.”
That decision potentially affected:
Specialized prosecutors;
Institutional knowledge;
Training programs;
International relationships;
Cross-district coordination;

Platform investigations;

Mixer and bridge expertise;

Digital-asset forfeiture;

Ransomware tracing;

Undercover operations;

Technical tools and contracts;
Source and witness relationships; and
Long-running investigations.
The investigation should determine whether NCET personnel were:
Reassigned within CCIPS;
Moved to unrelated matters;
Removed from DOJ;
Offered deferred resignation;
Stripped of database access;
Prevented from continuing existing cases; or
Permitted to retain cryptocurrency expertise informally.
Dissolution of the organizational label is not necessarily elimination of all underlying capacity. Personnel and workload records are required to establish the practical effect.
XII. Market Integrity and Major Frauds Unit

The directive also required the Market Integrity and Major Frauds Unit to cease cryptocurrency enforcement and redirect its attention to immigration and procurement fraud.

This potentially removed expertise involving:
Market manipulation;
Wash trading;
Spoofing;
Insider trading;
False volume;
Exchange misconduct;

Token-price manipulation;

Misrepresentations to investors; and

Coordinated trading schemes.

Traditional fraud statutes remained available, but the responsible unit was directed away from the field.

The investigation should identify which open market-integrity matters were terminated or reassigned and whether any involved major donors, exchanges, token issuers, or presidentially connected businesses.

XIII. Dispersal to U.S. Attorney’s Offices
The directive placed greater responsibility on individual U.S. Attorney’s Offices.
This creates possible weaknesses because local offices differ in:
Technical expertise;
Staffing;
Blockchain tools;
forfeiture capacity;
National-security access;
Experience with overseas exchanges;

ability to coordinate parallel proceedings;

grand-jury resources; and

willingness to pursue politically sensitive matters.

It also creates potential jurisdictional fragmentation. One office may pursue conduct another office treats as non-criminal or outside the new policy.

A decentralized structure needs:

A mandatory national referral system;

Shared digital-asset evidence standards;
Joint charging protocols;
Central wallet and entity intelligence;
Conflict-resolution procedures;
Parallel-agency coordination; and
Preservation of state and international partnerships.
XIV. Digital Asset Coordinator network
Before the policy reversal, NCET helped establish a Digital Asset Coordinator network of specialized federal prosecutors.
The investigation should determine whether the network continued after NCET’s dissolution and whether it retained:

Regular training;

central leadership;

secure intelligence sharing;
case consultation;
seizure assistance;
foreign-liaison support;
expert witnesses;
technical tools; and
national policy guidance.

If the network remained functional and adequately supported, it may reduce claims that DOJ deactivated digital-asset enforcement.

If it became nominal, unfunded, or inactive, NCET’s dissolution may have had broader operational consequences.

XV. Continued Tornado Cash prosecution

DOJ continued at least some major cryptocurrency cases after the April 2025 directive.

In August 2025, a federal jury convicted a Tornado Cash co-founder of conspiring to operate an unlicensed money-transmitting business, while not reaching unanimous verdicts on certain other counts. DOJ characterized the case as involving knowing transmission of criminal proceeds. DOJ Tornado Cash conviction announcement
This is significant exculpatory evidence. It shows that:
Mixer-related prosecution did not stop absolutely;
Knowledge and criminal proceeds remained central;
Existing cases could continue under the new framework; and
The directive did not create formal immunity for all infrastructure operators.
The case should be compared with matters that were closed to determine how the department applied the distinction between knowing facilitation and unwitting platform use.
XVI. Continuing scale of cryptocurrency fraud
In April 2025, the FBI reported that its 2024 Internet Crime Report contained 859,532 complaints involving total reported internet-crime losses exceeding $16 billion.

Cryptocurrency investment fraud generated more reported losses than any other category—over $6.5 billion. FBI 2024 Internet Crime Report announcement

These figures do not establish that all losses passed through criminally culpable platforms. They demonstrate that cryptocurrency-related fraud remained a major enforcement problem when DOJ eliminated its specialized national team.

The investigation should compare:

Reported losses;

Criminal referrals;

Investigations opened;

Indictments;

Convictions;
Assets frozen;
Victim restitution; and
Personnel assigned before and after April 2025.

XVII. Treasury’s illicit-finance findings

Treasury’s 2024 national risk assessments identified continuing threats involving:

Fraud;

Drug trafficking;

Cybercrime;

Human trafficking and smuggling;

Corruption;

Professional money laundering;
Terrorist financing;
Ransomware;
Sanctions evasion;
North Korean hacking; and
Digitization of payments.
Treasury reported that foreign terrorist financing could involve cash, registered money-services businesses, and, in some cases, virtual assets. It also identified exploitation of the digital economy by North Korean-linked actors. Treasury’s 2024 national risk assessments
The record therefore does not support the proposition that digital assets are the only or necessarily the predominant illicit-finance channel.

It does establish that removing specialist capacity during a period of expanding digital-finance threats required a documented risk assessment.

XVIII. USAID comparison and selective narrative

DOGE-10 addressed allegations that USAID was publicly blamed for waste, foreign assistance failures, or indirect support reaching dangerous actors while the administration simultaneously narrowed scrutiny of digital-asset intermediaries.

The comparison should be stated carefully:
Foreign-assistance programs can be misused or diverted;
Digital assets can facilitate terrorism, narcotics, sanctions evasion, trafficking, and cybercrime;
Neither channel should be immunized from evidence-based investigation;
Relative scale must be established through reliable financial data;
Criminal intent cannot be inferred from the mere operation of either channel; and
Enforcement should follow conduct and evidence rather than political usefulness.
The relevant allegation is selective enforcement: aggressively investigating one disfavored channel while institutionally weakening investigation of a politically supported private channel presenting overlapping risks.
That hypothesis requires budget, case, loss, and outcome comparisons.
XIX. Platform accountability versus strict liability
A defensible intermediary-liability framework should distinguish among four levels:

Platform conductAppropriate treatmentNo knowledge, reasonable compliance, prompt cooperationNo criminal liability for user conductNegligent or deficient complianceCivil or regulatory remediation, depending on lawRepeated red flags and conscious avoidanceInvestigation for willful blindness or applicable violationsKnowing facilitation, conspiracy, concealment, or profit sharingFull criminal investigation and prosecution where elements are met

The April 2025 policy is defensible insofar as it protects the first category.

It becomes problematic if prosecutors are discouraged from investigating facts necessary to distinguish the third and fourth categories from the first.

XX. Beneficiaries of the enforcement shift

Potential beneficiaries include:

Centralized exchanges;

Offshore exchanges serving U.S. customers;

Stablecoin issuers;
Mixer and privacy-service operators;
Noncustodial wallet providers;
DeFi developers and front-end operators;
Venture-capital portfolio companies;
Token issuers;

Market makers; and

Financial institutions providing services to those entities.

Benefit does not establish participation in the policy’s creation.

The investigation should determine whether any beneficiary:

Funded Fairshake;
Supported Stand With Crypto;
Donated to presidential or congressional political committees;
Invested in a presidentially affiliated cryptocurrency business;
Lobbied DOJ or the White House;
Requested NCET’s dissolution;
Supplied proposed memorandum language;
Received advance notice; or
Had an investigation closed under the directive.
XXI. White House and Working Group influence
The memorandum explicitly tied DOJ policy to Executive Order 14178 and committed DOJ to the President’s Working Group on Digital Asset Markets.
DOJ representatives were directed to:
Review regulations, guidance, and orders;

Develop recommendations affecting the sector;

Participate in regulatory and legislative proposals; and

Implement recommendations adopted by the President.

This created a formal connection between criminal-enforcement policy and a White House group chaired by the Special Advisor for AI and Crypto.

The investigation should determine whether the Working Group discussed:

Particular criminal subjects;
Pending grand-jury matters;
Named exchanges;
Mixers or wallet providers;
Political donors;
World Liberty Financial;
Binance or Changpeng Zhao;
Justin Sun or Tron;
Asset-forfeiture decisions; or
Closure of specific investigations.

Policy coordination is lawful. Disclosure of grand-jury or protected law-enforcement information to unauthorized private interests would raise separate concerns.

XXII. DOGE’s potential role

No public evidence presently establishes that DOGE ordered NCET’s dissolution.
A DOGE connection would require evidence that DOGE personnel:
Recommended eliminating the unit;
Identified its employees for removal;
Reduced access to tracing tools;
Cancelled analytical contracts;
Accessed case or forfeiture data;
Interfered with international cooperation;
Directed closure of investigations;

Targeted prosecutors or agents;

Shared information with private cryptocurrency interests; or

Used budget and personnel systems to make the April memorandum operationally irreversible.

The investigation should compare DOGE agency-access dates with:

NCET personnel decisions;
CCIPS restructuring;
contract cancellations;
loss of analytical capacity;

case closures; and

enforcement announcements.

Temporal overlap alone is insufficient.
XXIII. Deactivation versus reprioritization test
The practical effect should be assessed through measurable indicators:
IndicatorReprioritizationDeactivationSpecialist personnelReassigned to high-priority crypto casesRemoved or diverted entirelyOpen casesNarrowed using documented criteriaBroadly closed without individualized reviewTrainingPreserved through DAC/CCIPSSuspended or unfundedPlatform casesPursued when knowledge is provableAvoided categoricallySeizure capacityMaintained or increasedTools, contracts, and staff reducedInternational coordinationPreservedRelationships lapseFraud resultsStable or improvedIndictments and recovery declinePolitical connectionsNo effectConnected entities receive unusual relief
This comparison should be performed using fiscal-year and case-level data rather than rhetoric.
XXIV. Potential obstruction concerns
Policy change becomes potentially obstructive if officials acted corruptly to affect a specific pending matter.
Relevant evidence would include:
Identification of a particular investigation;
Knowledge that evidence implicated a political patron;

Direction to close, delay, or transfer the matter;

Concealment or destruction of evidence;

False explanations;

Interference with witnesses;

Retaliation against investigators;

Improper disclosure of investigative information; or
A financial or political benefit connected to the intervention.
A general memorandum prospectively changing enforcement policy does not, without more, establish obstruction.
XXV. Potential RICO relevance
NCET dissolution and enforcement retrenchment are not themselves RICO predicates.
Potential RICO significance could arise if the policy was used to facilitate or conceal qualifying crimes such as:
Bribery;
Honest-services wire fraud;
Money laundering;
Obstruction of justice;

Destruction or falsification of records;

Witness tampering;
Retaliation against witnesses;
Fraudulent concealment of political or financial benefits; or
Continuing criminal activity knowingly protected by public officials.
A valid theory would require defendant-specific proof that officials or private actors agreed to use governmental power to protect or advance an unlawful enterprise.
Policy alignment between government and industry is insufficient.

XXVI. Exculpatory and alternative evidence

A complete investigation must preserve evidence that:

DOJ has discretion to establish national prosecution priorities;

Criminal law should not substitute for unclear financial regulation;
Some licensing and classification questions were genuinely disputed;
The memorandum preserved fraud, terrorism, narcotics, trafficking, cartel, hacking, and sanctions cases;
Platform operators should not be strictly liable for users;
CCIPS retained digital-asset expertise;
U.S. Attorney’s Offices continued cryptocurrency prosecutions;
The Tornado Cash matter proceeded after NCET’s dissolution;
No named donor requested the policy;
No politically connected investigation received special treatment;
Staff and tools were preserved despite organizational restructuring; and
DOJ’s new approach improved focus on intentional criminal conduct.
These facts could demonstrate a lawful narrowing of enforcement rather than institutional sabotage.

DOGE-23 — SEC Enforcement Dismissals, Career-Staff Overrides, Coinbase and Binance Relief, and the Alleged Delivery of Regulatory Benefits to Political Patrons

I. Scope and investigative purpose

This exhibit examines the Securities and Exchange Commission’s dismissal, settlement, stay, or termination of significant cryptocurrency enforcement matters after the 2024 presidential election.

The principal subjects include:

Coinbase, Inc. and Coinbase Global, Inc.;
Binance Holdings Ltd., BAM Trading Services, and Changpeng Zhao;
Kraken and its Payward entities;
Consensys Software Inc.;
Ripple Labs;
Justin Sun, the Tron entities, and Rainberry;
SEC commissioners and political appointees;
SEC Division of Enforcement leadership;
Career attorneys and investigators;
White House digital-asset officials;
Fairshake contributors and affiliated political organizations;
DOGE and OMB personnel affecting SEC staffing or resources; and
Private attorneys, lobbyists, and intermediaries communicating with the government.

The operative question is whether the dismissals represented:

A lawful, generally applicable change in regulatory policy;
Litigation-risk management;
Implementation of an electoral mandate;
Selective political favoritism; or
Delivery of promised governmental benefits to campaign patrons.

II. Categories of enforcement relief

The phrase “SEC dropped a crypto case” can refer to several legally distinct events:

CategoryLegal effectClosed investigationSEC staff ends an investigation without filing chargesWells-process terminationStaff declines to recommend or pursue charges after noticeLitigation stayFiled case is paused but remains pendingDismissal without prejudiceSEC may potentially refile, subject to applicable lawDismissal with prejudiceClaims concerning the covered conduct cannot ordinarily be refiledSettlementSome claims or penalties remain while others are resolvedAppeal withdrawalExisting judgment may remain, but appellate review endsDisqualification waiverCollateral consequences of a judgment are removedRule or guidance rescissionChanges treatment across a broader category
Each matter must be evaluated from its official order, stipulation, docket, and commission authorization. Corporate press releases are not sufficient.
III. Enforcement-reversal chronology
DateSEC actionJanuary 21, 2025SEC created Crypto Task ForceFebruary 2025SEC agreed to dismiss Coinbase litigationMarch 2025SEC dismissed Kraken and Consensys casesMay 2025SEC dismissed Binance litigation with prejudice2025SEC resolved or withdrew additional cryptocurrency investigations and appealsAugust 2025SEC granted Ripple a Regulation D disqualification waiverMarch 2026SEC proposed resolving one Rainberry wash-trading claim while dismissing remaining claims against Justin Sun and the Tron defendants
The sequence demonstrates a sustained policy reversal rather than a single isolated litigation decision.
That pattern is relevant to regulatory-capture analysis. It does not alone establish that any dismissal was purchased.
IV. Coinbase litigation
A. Original allegations
The SEC filed its Coinbase action in June 2023. It alleged that Coinbase operated as an unregistered:
National securities exchange;
Broker;
Clearing agency; and
Provider of an unregistered staking program.
Coinbase disputed the SEC’s jurisdiction and maintained that the listed assets and services did not constitute securities transactions requiring the registrations alleged.
B. Judicial posture before dismissal
The district court had granted Coinbase’s motion for judgment on the pleadings in part but allowed substantial SEC claims to proceed.
The court later permitted Coinbase to seek an interlocutory appeal concerning important legal questions. Therefore, the action had not been conclusively resolved for either side when the Commission dismissed it.
C. Dismissal
On February 27, 2025, the SEC filed a joint stipulation dismissing the Coinbase case with prejudice concerning conduct alleged through the filing date.
The SEC expressly stated:
The dismissal would facilitate reform of its regulatory approach;
The decision was an exercise of enforcement discretion;
It was based on policy rather than an assessment of the claims’ merits; and

It did not necessarily reflect the Commission’s position in another case.

SEC Coinbase dismissal announcement

The stipulation also required Coinbase to withdraw its interlocutory appeal and release specified claims for fees or other relief against the government. Coinbase joint dismissal stipulation
V. Coinbase’s political and financial connections
Coinbase was not merely a regulated defendant. It also:
Contributed substantial funds to Fairshake;
Publicly committed an additional $25 million for later election activity;
Helped launch Stand With Crypto;
Lobbied Congress, the White House, Treasury, SEC, and CFTC;
Advocated FIT21, CLARITY, and stablecoin legislation;
Opposed “regulation by enforcement”; and
Publicly credited the industry’s political strategy with helping produce a pro-crypto Congress.
These facts establish motive, access, and benefit. They do not establish that Coinbase’s contributions purchased dismissal of its case.
The evidentiary bridge would require communications showing that:
Dismissal was discussed with candidates or campaign officials;
Political support was conditioned on litigation relief;
White House personnel instructed the SEC to terminate the matter;
Coinbase or Fairshake representatives received nonpublic assurances;
SEC decision-makers had undisclosed Coinbase interests;
Similarly situated non-contributors were treated differently; or
Career recommendations were overridden for political reasons.

VI. Commissioner Crenshaw’s Coinbase dissent

Commissioner Caroline Crenshaw publicly objected to the Coinbase dismissal.

She described it as an unprecedented mid-litigation reversal and emphasized that:

The district court had allowed central SEC claims to proceed;

The Commission dismissed the matter based on possible future policy;

Existing law had not yet been replaced;

The action risked unequal treatment;
The dismissal undermined the credibility of enforcement personnel; and
The timing could create the appearance that enforcement was being aligned with election cycles or actors possessing significant means.
Commissioner Crenshaw’s dissent

Her statement is direct evidence of disagreement within the Commission. It does not establish that career staff were overruled or that bribery occurred. It does establish that the policy rationale and institutional consequences were contested internally.

VII. Binance litigation

A. Original case

In June 2023, the SEC sued Binance Holdings, BAM Trading, BAM Management, and founder Changpeng Zhao.

The SEC alleged, among other matters, violations involving:

Unregistered exchange, broker, and clearing functions;

Offers and sales of crypto-asset securities;

Misrepresentations concerning https://t.co/yYBxs2hCol;
Customer-asset controls;
Trading-platform operations; and
Certain market-integrity practices.

Binance and the other defendants disputed the claims.

B. Dismissal with prejudice

On May 29, 2025, the SEC and the Binance defendants filed a joint stipulation dismissing the civil enforcement action with prejudice.

The SEC stated that it considered dismissal appropriate as an exercise of discretion and “as a policy matter.” It again cautioned that the decision did not necessarily represent the Commission’s position in another proceeding. SEC Binance dismissal record

Because the dismissal was with prejudice, the SEC surrendered the ability to pursue the dismissed claims concerning the covered conduct in the same form.

VIII. Binance’s separate criminal and regulatory history
The SEC dismissal must not be confused with Binance’s separate resolution with DOJ, Treasury, FinCEN, and OFAC.
A civil SEC dismissal does not automatically:
Vacate a criminal conviction;
Eliminate a DOJ plea agreement;
Remove FinCEN monitoring;
Cancel sanctions-compliance obligations;

Overturn another agency’s findings; or

Establish that Binance’s earlier conduct was lawful.

The investigation should identify precisely which obligations remained in effect and whether later executive actions, clemency, waivers, or monitoring changes altered them.
Combining distinct proceedings risks overstating the relief delivered by the SEC.
IX. Binance, World Liberty Financial, and foreign-capital concerns
Binance-related relief requires heightened review because other exhibits identify alleged financial intersections involving:
Binance;
Changpeng Zhao;
World Liberty Financial;

USD1;

Foreign purchasers or investors;

UAE-linked capital; and

Presidential clemency or regulatory interests.

The necessary inquiry is whether any Binance-linked person or entity:
Purchased or facilitated acquisition of a Trump-family-linked token;
Used USD1 in a transaction benefiting World Liberty Financial;
Sought clemency or regulatory relief;
Communicated with White House officials;
Discussed the SEC case with political intermediaries;

Provided something of value before dismissal; or

Received relief after a specific promise or understanding.

No quid pro quo should be alleged without establishing the persons, transactions, communications, timing, and requested official acts.

X. Kraken dismissal

The SEC sued Kraken’s Payward entities in November 2023, alleging that the platform operated as an unregistered exchange, broker, dealer, and clearing agency.

The SEC also alleged deficient recordkeeping, commingling, and customer risks. Kraken denied wrongdoing.

On March 27, 2025, the SEC dismissed the action with prejudice. It stated that dismissal would facilitate reform of its regulatory approach and did not rest on an assessment of the merits. SEC Kraken dismissal

The dismissal should be evaluated against:
Kraken political contributions;
Lobbying contacts;
Participation in White House or SEC policy meetings;
Fairshake or related financing;
Communications with commissioners;

Career litigation recommendations; and

Treatment of the earlier staking settlement.

XI. Consensys dismissal

The SEC sued Consensys in June 2024 concerning MetaMask-related brokerage and staking services.

On March 27, 2025, the Commission dismissed the action with prejudice, again stating that the decision was meant to facilitate reform of cryptocurrency policy and was not based on the merits. SEC Consensys dismissal

The investigation should determine:

Whether the dismissal covered all originally alleged conduct;

Whether investor restitution remained available;

Whether Consensys participated in lobbying or political financing;

Whether it received advance notice;
Whether MetaMask services changed;
Whether similar non-crypto intermediaries received comparable treatment; and
Whether developer or DeFi provisions in CLARITY tracked Consensys’s litigation positions.
XII. Ripple relief
Ripple’s matter followed a different procedural course.

The district court entered a final judgment in August 2024 that included an injunction arising from Ripple’s violation of Securities Act §5. Both sides pursued appellate proceedings.

In 2025, the parties attempted to obtain judicial relief modifying or dissolving parts of the final judgment, but the district court declined the requested procedure. The appeals were later dismissed while the existing injunction remained.

On August 8, 2025, the SEC granted Ripple a waiver from the Regulation D “bad actor” disqualification that otherwise resulted from the injunction. The Commission found good cause for the waiver. SEC Ripple waiver order

This was regulatory relief, but it was not a complete erasure of the final judgment. Ripple remained subject to the injunction while receiving relief from an important collateral fundraising consequence.

XIII. Ripple’s Fairshake funding

Ripple publicly announced a total of approximately $50 million in Fairshake contributions across 2023 and 2024.
The sequence therefore includes:
Major political contributions;
Election of a more crypto-friendly Congress and administration;
SEC policy reversal;
Attempted resolution of appellate litigation; and
Grant of a regulatory disqualification waiver.

This chronology warrants examination. It does not prove an exchange.

The investigation should obtain:
Ripple communications with Fairshake;
Communications with SEC commissioners or staff;
White House contacts;
Waiver-request materials;
Staff recommendations;
Comparable waiver precedents;

Commission voting records;

Lobbying disclosures; and

Communications discussing the relationship between political spending and litigation treatment.

XIV. Justin Sun and Tron resolution

In March 2026, the SEC announced a proposed global resolution involving Justin Sun, Tron Foundation, BitTorrent Foundation, and Rainberry.

Under the proposed resolution:

Rainberry would settle a Securities Act wash-trading claim;

Rainberry would pay a $10 million civil penalty;
Remaining claims against Rainberry would be dismissed;
All claims against Justin Sun, Tron Foundation, and BitTorrent Foundation would be dismissed with prejudice; and
A separate claim against DeAndre Cortez Way would be voluntarily dismissed.
SEC Tron resolution
This resolution did not eliminate every allegation without consequence: it retained a wash-trading settlement and civil penalty against Rainberry. But it delivered substantial relief to Sun and the other Tron defendants.
XV. Justin Sun and World Liberty Financial nexus

DOGE-16 identified Justin Sun’s reported investments in World Liberty Financial and his potential financial interest in Trump-family-linked cryptocurrency ventures.

The relevant sequence must be investigated carefully:

Date and value of each investment;

Wallet source;

Beneficial owner;

Any advisory or governance role;
Communications with Trump-family representatives;
SEC litigation stays or settlement discussions;
White House contacts;
Political contributions;
Market-value changes; and
Final terms of the March 2026 resolution.

A large investment in a presidentially affiliated business followed by governmental relief can support a corruption inquiry. It does not establish bribery unless evidence demonstrates a corrupt exchange involving official action.

XVI. Career-staff override theory

The title references alleged career-staff overrides, but the public record presently provides limited direct evidence concerning internal recommendations.

A career-staff override would mean that:

Enforcement attorneys or investigators recommended continuing a case;

Political leadership rejected that recommendation;

The decision was made for reasons unrelated to neutral enforcement policy or litigation merits; and

A politically connected party received the resulting benefit.

Necessary evidence includes:

Action memoranda;

Recommendation memoranda;

Case-closing reports;
Commissioner briefing materials;
Meeting notes;
Emails and messages;
Recorded votes;

Staff objections;

Whistleblower disclosures;

Resignation statements;
Recusal records; and
Communications with the White House or defendants.
Commissioner Crenshaw’s objections show disagreement at the commission level. They do not prove what career staff recommended.
XVII. Commission authority and enforcement discretion
The SEC possesses broad discretion concerning whether to:
Investigate;
File charges;
Settle;

Dismiss;

Appeal;

Issue a waiver; or

Reallocate enforcement resources.

A new commission may lawfully change policy, including deciding that some questions should be resolved by rulemaking or Congress instead of litigation.
The critical limitations are that the Commission may not lawfully exercise discretion:
In exchange for a bribe;
Because of an undisclosed personal financial interest;
To confer selective benefits on political patrons without a legitimate basis;

To obstruct another lawful investigation;

Through knowingly false official explanations;

In violation of applicable ethics obligations; or

Through prohibited White House interference with an independent agency.

XVIII. Policy consistency analysis

A legitimate policy change should ordinarily produce consistent treatment of similarly situated parties.

Investigators should compare relief based on:
Nature of allegations;
Procedural posture;
Judicial rulings;
Presence of fraud or scienter allegations;
Customer-asset harm;
Registration-only theories;
Staking claims;
Exchange, broker, dealer, or clearing claims;
Wash trading;

Prior settlements;

Political contributions;

White House access; and

Participation in industry policy initiatives.

If relief tracked legal category rather than political connection, that would support a neutral policy explanation.

If major donors received dismissal while comparable non-donors remained under enforcement, selective-treatment concerns would increase.
XIX. Absence of merits determinations
The SEC repeatedly stated that its 2025 dismissals did not rest on an assessment of the merits.
This language has two important consequences:

The dismissals should not be represented as findings that defendants were innocent or that the earlier complaints were false.

The Commission knowingly surrendered pending claims for policy reasons without resolving whether the alleged conduct violated existing law.
That makes the internal policy decision—not merely litigation risk—the central subject of this investigation.
XX. Investor and public harm analysis
Potential public injury from enforcement dismissals includes:
Loss of judicial clarification;
Reduced deterrence;
Elimination of possible disgorgement;
Loss of civil penalties;
Reduced investor restitution;
Unequal treatment of regulated competitors;
Impaired state enforcement;
Increased market concentration;

Loss of evidence through delay; and

Future reliance on conduct effectively left unadjudicated.

Potential public benefits include:

Ending legally uncertain litigation;

Conserving agency resources;
Allowing Congress to establish clearer rules;
Preventing inconsistent judicial decisions;
Encouraging domestic innovation;
Creating more workable registration pathways; and
Replacing fragmented enforcement with generally applicable rules.
A complete damages analysis must account for both.

XXI. White House interference inquiry

Executive Order 14178 directed agencies to review digital-asset regulations and participate in the President’s Working Group. It did not expressly order the SEC to dismiss Coinbase, Binance, Kraken, Consensys, Ripple, or Tron matters.

The investigation should determine whether White House personnel:
Discussed specific defendants;
Requested litigation pauses;
Transmitted donor concerns;
Sought dismissal before public policy review;
Received advance notice;
Coordinated announcement timing;

Connected dismissal to legislation;

Discussed presidential-family financial interests; or

Pressured commissioners through appointments, budgets, or personnel decisions.

General White House advocacy for pro-crypto policy is not the same as intervention in a particular enforcement matter.

XXII. DOGE nexus
DOGE’s potential role would require proof that its personnel:
Identified SEC enforcement attorneys for removal;
Reduced resources assigned to cryptocurrency matters;
Accessed enforcement or litigation databases;
Communicated with defendants or their investors;
Recommended dismissal or reassignment;
Interfered with document preservation;
Targeted agency personnel who resisted policy changes;
Influenced SEC contracting or blockchain-analysis capacity; or
Used White House access to connect private interests with commission decision-makers.
No public evidence presently establishes that DOGE directed any of the identified dismissals.
Workforce reduction and regulatory retreat may be consistent with a common administration agenda without constituting a coordinated criminal agreement.

XXIII. Campaign-patron benefit matrix

The investigation should construct a standardized matrix:

