Other crimes enumerated in 18 U.S.C. §1961.
A prosecutable theory would require identifying:
The payer or benefit provider;
The recipient;
The thing of value;
The requested official act;
The agreement or understanding;

The execution of the act;

The relevant interstate communications; and
The defendant’s corrupt intent.
Political donations and later favorable policy do not supply these elements automatically.
XXV. Exculpatory and alternative evidence
A balanced record must preserve evidence that:
The industry presented legitimate unresolved legal questions;
Courts had issued differing or incomplete cryptocurrency rulings;

The SEC intended to replace litigation with rulemaking;

Dismissals followed a category-based policy;

Defendants surrendered appeals or claims against the government;

Commission decisions were formally authorized;

Career staff agreed with dismissal;

White House officials did not intervene in particular cases;
Commissioners and staff complied with ethics requirements;
Political contributions were independent and publicly disclosed;
Relief was not limited to major donors;
Fraud cases continued after the policy change; and
The Commission preserved authority over manipulation and investor deception.
These facts could establish a lawful policy reversal even if the industry obtained major financial benefits.

DOGE-22 — Crypto-Friendly Appointments, Agency Personnel Purges, Ethics Waivers, and the Alleged Installation of an Industry-Aligned Regulatory Government

I. Scope and investigative purpose

This exhibit examines whether the administration used appointments, removals, reassignments, workforce reductions, ethics determinations, and enforcement-policy changes to replace independent cryptocurrency oversight with an executive-controlled and industry-aligned regulatory structure.

The principal subjects include:

The White House Special Advisor for AI and Crypto;
The President’s Working Group on Digital Asset Markets;
Securities and Exchange Commission leadership;
Commodity Futures Trading Commission leadership;
Treasury, FinCEN, OCC, FDIC, and Federal Reserve personnel;
Department of Justice leadership and cryptocurrency-enforcement units;
DOGE, USDS, OMB, and agency-reorganization personnel;
Industry executives, investors, attorneys, and lobbyists entering government;
Departing officials subsequently employed by cryptocurrency interests; and
Officials receiving ethics waivers, recusals, or special-government-employee designations.

The phrase “installation of an industry-aligned regulatory government” is an investigative characterization, not a presently established criminal conclusion.

II. Executive reorientation of federal policy

On January 23, 2025, President Donald J. Trump issued Executive Order 14178, Strengthening American Leadership in Digital Financial Technology.

The order made support for digital assets an express policy of the executive branch. It directed the government to:

Protect access to public blockchain networks;

Promote lawful dollar-backed stablecoins;
Protect cryptocurrency companies’ access to banking;
Develop clearer jurisdictional boundaries;
Prohibit federal development or promotion of a central-bank digital currency;
Revoke the prior administration’s digital-asset executive order;
Review existing regulations, guidance, and orders for possible rescission or modification; and
Develop legislative and regulatory proposals favorable to the new policy.
The order established the President’s Working Group on Digital Asset Markets inside the National Economic Council and placed the Special Advisor for AI and Crypto in the chair. Its members included the heads of Treasury, DOJ, Commerce, DHS, OMB, the SEC, and the CFTC. Executive Order 14178

This was not merely an appointment of several crypto-friendly officials. It created a government-wide policy-coordination mechanism capable of directing review across nearly every agency with digital-asset jurisdiction.

III. Centralization through the President’s Working Group

The Working Group was directed to identify regulations, guidance documents, orders, and other federal actions affecting the digital-asset sector.

The relevant agencies were then required to recommend whether those materials should be:

Rescinded;

Modified;

Preserved;
Replaced with regulations; or
Supplemented with new legislative proposals.
The Working Group also received responsibility for developing:
A federal digital-asset market framework;

Stablecoin recommendations;

Consumer-protection proposals;
Risk-management policies;
A possible national digital-asset stockpile; and
Criteria for retaining cryptocurrency seized through law enforcement.
This arrangement placed regulatory agencies and DOJ enforcement policy within a coordinated executive structure chaired by a political adviser.
That coordination may be lawful. The concern is whether independent agency judgment was displaced by White House, donor, or industry instructions.

IV. David Sacks and special-government-employee status

David Sacks was appointed White House Special Advisor for AI and Crypto and chaired the President’s Working Group.

His background in venture capital, technology companies, cryptocurrency investment, and political fundraising created potential conflicts requiring careful financial disclosure, divestiture, recusal, and screening.

The White House issued a March 5, 2025 memorandum addressing Sacks’s role and ethical status. The document should be analyzed with:

His public financial disclosures;

All identified digital-asset holdings;

Venture-fund interests;

Limited-partner arrangements;
Management or carried-interest rights;
Investments held through Craft Ventures;
Spousal and dependent-family interests;
Divestiture dates;
Valuation dates;
Recusal obligations;
White House ethics opinions; and
Any waiver or authorization permitting participation.
White House memorandum concerning David Sacks
Special-government-employee status does not eliminate conflict-of-interest laws. Its practical effect depends upon:
Number of government-service days;

Scope of assigned duties;

Matters personally and substantially handled;

Continued private employment;
Compensation arrangements;
Particular matters affecting identified financial interests; and
Whether recusals were followed operationally.
V. Why SGE status matters
A special government employee may continue substantial private activities while serving the government on a limited basis. That arrangement can provide valuable expertise, but it can also blur lines between public duty and private investment.
The investigation should determine:

Whether Sacks continued managing, advising, or communicating with private investment interests;

Whether he participated in decisions affecting portfolio companies or token holdings;

Whether working-group meetings involved particular companies rather than broad policy;

Whether industry representatives contacted him through private channels;

Whether government workdays were accurately recorded;
Whether he exceeded the service level contemplated by his designation;
Whether recusals were documented in meeting records;
Whether subordinate officials understood the recusals; and
Whether divestitures occurred before policy announcements capable of affecting market prices.
A general policy affecting an entire industry may be treated differently from a particular matter affecting specifically identified companies. The factual classification must be made policy by policy and meeting by meeting.
VI. Paul Atkins and SEC leadership
Paul S. Atkins became SEC Chairman on April 21, 2025 after Senate confirmation.
The SEC’s official biography states that Atkins founded Patomak Global Partners and helped develop best practices for the digital-asset sector. He previously served as an SEC commissioner and worked in securities and financial-services consulting. SEC appointment announcement

Atkins publicly criticized the prior SEC’s approach and endorsed a “rational, fit-for-purpose” regulatory framework for cryptocurrency markets. He praised Commissioner Hester Peirce’s crypto-policy work and committed to working with the administration and Congress. Atkins’s April 2025 Crypto Task Force remarks

These positions demonstrate a clear policy orientation. They do not demonstrate corruption.

The investigation should examine:

Patomak’s cryptocurrency clients;

Atkins’s former clients and compensation;

His financial holdings and divestitures;

Recusal agreements;

Participation in matters affecting former clients;

Contacts with Fairshake donors;
Contacts with World Liberty Financial or its affiliates;
Communications concerning pending SEC cases;
His role in enforcement dismissals; and
Whether career staff recommendations were followed or overridden.
VII. SEC Crypto Task Force
Before Atkins’s confirmation, Acting SEC Chair Mark Uyeda established a Crypto Task Force led by Commissioner Hester Peirce.
The task force was charged with:
Developing a clearer regulatory framework;
Identifying registration pathways;

Drawing jurisdictional boundaries;

Providing disclosure frameworks;

Engaging with industry participants; and

Reconsidering the prior enforcement-centered approach.

An expert task force is not evidence of capture. The inquiry should determine whether access was balanced among:
Cryptocurrency companies;
Investors and consumers;
State regulators;
law-enforcement agencies;
Academic experts;

cybersecurity specialists;

fraud victims;
financial institutions; and
national-security authorities.
A task force dominated by industry representatives while excluding enforcement personnel and victims could generate an appearance of predetermined policy.
VIII. SEC enforcement dismissals and leadership transition
During 2025, the SEC dismissed, paused, or withdrew several significant cryptocurrency proceedings, including matters involving Coinbase, Kraken, Consensys, Binance, and other industry participants.
In dismissing the Coinbase civil enforcement action, the SEC stated that its decision reflected its policy judgment and did not express a position on the merits of the allegations. This distinction is important: the agency did not establish that the original case lacked legal or factual support.
The investigation should obtain for each terminated matter:
Career staff recommendations;

Commissioner voting records;

Dismissal memoranda;

Communications with defendants;

White House or working-group communications;

Recusal records;

Settlement proposals;

Litigation-risk analysis;
Any political-appointee override;
Timing relative to campaign support; and
Whether similarly situated non-donor defendants received comparable relief.
Policy-based dismissal can be lawful prosecutorial discretion. Selective dismissal tied to political support or personal benefit would present a different issue.
IX. SEC workforce reductions
The administration’s FY2026 planning contemplated a materially smaller SEC workforce than FY2024 levels, including reductions affecting enforcement and investment-management personnel.
A workforce reduction does not establish a targeted purge. Attrition, retirement programs, budget priorities, and voluntary departures must be separated from politically directed removals.
The investigation should determine:
Total positions eliminated;
Vacancies left unfilled;

Voluntary versus involuntary departures;

Divisions disproportionately affected;

Cryptocurrency expertise lost;

Investigations delayed or terminated;

Whether departing personnel had worked on donor-related cases;

Whether enforcement attorneys were reassigned;

Whether records and leads were preserved;
Whether DOGE recommended the changes; and
Whether industry representatives requested particular staffing reductions.
The proper factual terminology is workforce reduction or attrition unless evidence establishes that employees were selected for removal because of protected activity, enforcement work, or political disfavor.
X. CFTC leadership under Michael Selig
Michael S. Selig was nominated in October 2025, confirmed by the Senate on December 18, and sworn in as CFTC Chairman on December 22, 2025. CFTC appointment announcement
The appointment was particularly significant because FIT21 and the CLARITY framework contemplated giving the CFTC extensive jurisdiction over digital-commodity spot markets.
The investigation should examine:
Selig’s prior private clients and digital-asset work;
Communications with Fairshake donors;
Financial interests;

Recusal obligations;

Participation in drafting market-structure legislation;

Appointment lobbying;

Personnel selected for CFTC leadership;

Use of provisional registration and exemptive authority;

Treatment of existing exchange listings; and

Coordination with the White House Working Group.
The combination of a crypto-friendly chair and expanded statutory jurisdiction does not prove capture. The relevant question is whether the appointment process or later official acts involved undisclosed commitments or conflicts.
XI. CFTC staffing and rulemaking capacity
Transferring large digital-asset markets to the CFTC without providing proportional resources could create nominal regulation without effective enforcement.
Investigators should compare:
New responsibilities assigned;
Authorized versus appropriated funding;
Staffing before and after market-structure legislation;
Enforcement personnel;
Technological and blockchain-analysis capacity;

Examination authority;

Self-regulatory-organization dependence;

Industry-funded fees;

Registration backlogs;

Foreign-platform supervision; and

State and federal information sharing.
A regulatory framework can be weakened not only through express exemptions but also through assigning extensive responsibilities to an understaffed agency.
XII. DOJ’s “Ending Regulation by Prosecution” directive
On April 7, 2025, Deputy Attorney General Todd Blanche issued a memorandum entitled Ending Regulation by Prosecution.
The memorandum:
Declared the digital-asset industry important to national economic development;
Directed prosecutors away from cases functioning as regulatory enforcement;
Narrowed potential cases involving exchanges, mixers, wallets, licensing, and registration;
Directed that inconsistent investigations be closed;
Required heightened approval for certain digital-asset theories;
Directed the Market Integrity and Major Frauds Unit to cease cryptocurrency enforcement; and

Disbanded the National Cryptocurrency Enforcement Team immediately.

DOJ memorandum

This represents a documented reduction and redirection of institutional enforcement capacity—not merely a change in public rhetoric.

XIII. The platform-liability omission

The DOJ memorandum preserved priority cases involving:

Investor fraud;
Embezzlement;
Rug pulls;
Hacking;
Cartels;
Narcotics;
Human trafficking;

Terrorism;

Organized crime; and

Sanctions-evasion conduct.

However, it stated that DOJ would pursue the individuals and enterprises committing those crimes while generally declining to pursue platforms merely because criminals used them.

That distinction may appropriately protect innocent intermediaries. It may also create a significant enforcement gap where a platform:

Ignores repeated warnings;
Designs services to avoid detection;
Profits from criminal volume;
Fails to implement required AML controls;
Assists high-risk customers;
Conceals beneficial ownership;
Provides direct support after notice; or
Deliberately structures itself to claim lack of knowledge.
The memorandum should not be treated as immunity for knowing participation. Investigators must determine how “unwitting” conduct was distinguished from deliberate blindness or willful assistance.
XIV. Disbandment of NCET

The National Cryptocurrency Enforcement Team was designed to consolidate expertise involving:

Cryptocurrency exchanges;

Mixers and tumblers;
Infrastructure providers;
ransomware;
Money laundering;
Tracing and forfeiture; and
Cross-border digital-asset investigations.
Its immediate disbandment dispersed specialized personnel and reassigned responsibility to ordinary U.S. Attorney’s Offices and existing components.
The investigation should determine:

Which NCET matters were open;

Which were closed;

Which were transferred;

Whether case evidence was preserved;

Whether international partners were notified;
Whether expertise was lost through resignation;
Whether donor-related companies appeared in any matter;
Whether DOGE or the White House participated in the decision;
Whether industry lobbyists requested dissolution; and
Whether victim or national-security investigations were impaired.
The public memorandum establishes the dissolution. It does not establish that the purpose was to protect any particular company.

XV. Treasury, FinCEN, OCC, and banking-regulator appointments

Cryptocurrency policy depends not only on the SEC and CFTC but also on:

Treasury;
FinCEN;
Office of the Comptroller of the Currency;
FDIC;
Federal Reserve;
IRS Criminal Investigation;
OFAC; and
Financial Stability Oversight Council.
The investigation should examine appointments affecting:
Bank access for cryptocurrency companies;

Stablecoin reserves;

Custody;

Suspicious-activity reporting;

Sanctions compliance;

Money-transmitter regulation;
Beneficial-ownership reporting;
Capital requirements;
Charter approvals; and
Supervision of foreign issuers.
For every appointee, the record should identify former employers, clients, token interests, compensation, political contributions, transition-team participation, recusals, and matters affecting previous clients.
XVI. Inspector-general removals
The administration removed multiple inspectors general in January 2025. Those removals affected the government-wide system responsible for investigating waste, abuse, conflicts, contracting irregularities, and misconduct.

An inspector-general removal is not automatically connected to cryptocurrency policy or DOGE.

The relevant questions are whether any removed or replacement inspector general had jurisdiction over:
DOGE access to agency systems;
Cryptocurrency-related contracts;
Treasury payment data;
Enforcement suppression;
Conflicts involving special government employees;
White House digital-asset policy;
Procurement by crypto-linked companies; or
Retaliation against agency whistleblowers.
The investigation must identify a specific obstructed audit, complaint, referral, or inquiry before alleging that an inspector-general removal furthered the cryptocurrency influence network.

XVII. Agency counsel and ethics-office independence

Political appointees frequently depend upon designated agency ethics officials to evaluate recusals, divestitures, and waivers.

Investigators should determine whether ethics offices:

Received complete financial information;

Had adequate staffing;

Were permitted to interview officials;

Issued written determinations;
Monitored compliance;
Could access wallet and token information;
Verified divestitures independently;
Tracked private fund holdings;
Examined family interests;
Preserved advice and communications; and
Were pressured to approve participation.

A waiver may be lawful and publicly justified. A secret or inadequately reasoned waiver affecting a donor or official’s financial interest deserves heightened scrutiny.

XVIII. Cryptocurrency-specific disclosure problems

Traditional financial-disclosure systems may be inadequate for digital assets because officials can hold value through:

Self-custodied wallets;

Foreign exchanges;

Private token warrants;
Simple Agreements for Future Tokens;
Venture funds;
Decentralized autonomous organizations;
Liquidity-pool interests;
Staking positions;
Wrapped assets;
Stablecoins;
NFTs;
Meme coins;
Trusts and family entities; and

Anonymous or pseudonymous wallets.

A disclosure listing only “cryptocurrency” or a venture-fund interest may not identify which official acts affect the asset’s value.

Investigators should require asset-specific, wallet-aware disclosure subject to privacy protections and independent verification.

XIX. Ethics waivers and recusals

For every official participating in digital-asset policy, the evidentiary file should include:
Appointment documents;
Status as employee, SGE, adviser, detailee, or contractor;
Financial disclosure;
Ethics agreement;
Certificate of compliance;
Divestiture records;
Recusal memorandum;
Waiver under applicable conflict rules;
Meeting-screening procedures;
Written advice concerning particular matters;
Calendar and participant records; and
Subsequent employment negotiations.
The investigation must distinguish:

Recusal: The official does not participate;

Divestiture: The conflicting interest is sold or relinquished;

Waiver: Participation is authorized despite a potential conflict;

Qualified diversified fund treatment: The interest may be treated as sufficiently diversified; and

General policy determination: The matter may be considered too broadly applicable to constitute a particular matter affecting specific parties.

The label used by an ethics office should be tested against the official’s actual participation.
XX. Enforcement-personnel “purge” standard
The term purge should be reserved for evidence showing that employees were removed, reassigned, pressured to resign, or denied access because they:
Investigated politically connected cryptocurrency companies;
Refused to dismiss cases;
Reported conflicts;
Protected evidence;
Resisted DOGE access;
Communicated with inspectors general;
Made protected whistleblower disclosures; or
Declined politically directed enforcement decisions.
Evidence could include:

Termination notices;

Reassignment memoranda;
Performance records;
Comparator employees;
Communications mentioning cases or political views;
Threats;

revoked credentials;

severance offers;

non-disclosure demands; and

Temporal proximity to protected activity.

General workforce reduction alone should not be pleaded as retaliatory removal.
XXI. Appointment-to-benefit chronology
A comprehensive analysis should use the following structure:
Appointment or personnel actionPrior industry connectionSubsequent government actionIdentified beneficiaryEvidence of recusal or participationWhite House crypto adviserInvestment or technology interestsExecutive policy and reserve recommendationsDigital-asset sectorTo be establishedSEC leadership changePrivate regulatory consultingTask force and enforcement reversalsExchanges and issuersTo be establishedCFTC leadership changeDigital-asset policy experienceExpanded commodity frameworkExchanges and platformsTo be establishedDOJ policy changePolitical appointmentNCET dissolution and charging limitsPlatforms and intermediariesNot ordinarily a financial-conflict issueAgency staffing reductionsDOGE/OMB reviewReduced examination or enforcement capacityRegulated entities generallyCausal nexus required
This chronology identifies issues for investigation. It does not establish that an appointment was made in exchange for campaign support.
XXII. DOGE’s potential operational role
DOGE could be relevant if it supplied the administrative mechanism for implementing a broader deregulatory program through:
Headcount reductions;

Hiring freezes;

Elimination of enforcement positions;
Contract cancellations;
Access to agency personnel systems;
Identification of employees for reassignment;
Reduction of investigative technology;
Closure of field offices;
Modification of data-access privileges;
Reorganization of agency components; or
Centralized review of regulatory work.
The investigation should determine whether DOGE personnel received lists of:

Cryptocurrency investigators;

Enforcement attorneys;

blockchain analysts;

Inspectors-general personnel;
FinCEN analysts;
Procurement-integrity personnel; and
Employees involved in donor-related matters.
Without evidence of targeted selection or intent, government-wide reductions cannot be attributed to a cryptocurrency protection scheme.
XXIII. Indicators of regulatory capture
Evidence supporting a regulatory-capture conclusion would include:
Appointments negotiated with major campaign contributors;
Donor veto power over nominees;
Undisclosed financial interests;
Selective ethics waivers;
Participation in former-client matters;
Instructions to dismiss named cases;
Removal of career officials who objected;
Destruction or concealment of investigative records;
Defunding enforcement without reducing statutory obligations;
Exclusive industry access to rulemaking;
Retaliation against whistleblowers;
Preferential treatment of presidential-family businesses; and
Government actions matching private commitments made during fundraising.
Evidence supporting ordinary policy change would include:
Senate confirmation;
Complete disclosures and divestitures;
effective recusals;
Transparent public rulemaking;
Neutral treatment of similarly situated entities;
Documented legal analysis;
Preservation of fraud and national-security enforcement;
Balanced stakeholder participation; and
No communications linking appointments or dismissals to political support.
XXIV. Potential criminal and RICO analysis
Appointments, policy changes, workforce reductions, and enforcement discretion are not RICO predicates by themselves.
Potentially qualifying conduct could arise if evidence establishes:
Bribery involving an appointment or official act;
Honest-services fraud based on bribery or kickbacks;
Wire fraud through intentionally false financial disclosures;

Concealment of token or business interests;

Money laundering of compensation or political benefits;

Obstruction of pending investigations;

Destruction or falsification of agency records;

Witness tampering;
Retaliation connected to a qualifying obstruction offense; or
Other offenses enumerated in 18 U.S.C. §1961.
A valid RICO theory would require proof that identified defendants knowingly conducted the affairs of an enterprise through a pattern of qualifying crimes. Shared deregulatory preferences are not enough.

DOGE-16 — Justin Sun, Tron, World Liberty Financial Investments, SEC Litigation Relief, and the Alleged Purchase of Regulatory Favor

I. Investigative purpose

This exhibit examines whether cryptocurrency entrepreneur Justin Sun or affiliated entities supplied financial benefits to businesses associated with President Donald J. Trump and subsequently received favorable treatment from the Securities and Exchange Commission or other federal authorities.

The investigation focuses on:

Sun’s purchases of World Liberty Financial tokens;
his appointment as a World Liberty Financial adviser;
his purchases and holdings of the $TRUMP meme coin;
his access to President Trump through the May 2025 token-holder dinner;
the SEC’s February 2025 agreement to stay its litigation against Sun;
the SEC’s March 2026 settlement dismissing every claim against Sun, the Tron Foundation, and the BitTorrent Foundation;
Rainberry’s limited settlement and $10 million penalty;
the establishment of a publicly traded TRX treasury company;
relationships among Sun, Trump-family business interests, Binance, World Liberty Financial, and associated financial intermediaries; and
whether these events resulted from ordinary policy change, litigation compromise, regulatory capture, or a corrupt exchange.

The chronology creates a substantial factual basis for investigation. It does not independently establish that Sun purchased regulatory relief.

II. Principal persons and entities

Potential subjects, witnesses, and record custodians include:

