foreign exchanges, custodians, and beneficial owners involved in the relevant transfers.

Inclusion does not establish wrongdoing.

III. SEC’s original case against Sun

On March 22, 2023, the SEC charged Justin Sun and three affiliated companies:

Tron Foundation Limited;
BitTorrent Foundation Ltd.; and
Rainberry Inc., formerly BitTorrent Inc.

The SEC alleged:

unregistered offers and sales of TRX and BTT;
use of bounty programs and airdrops to distribute the tokens;
fraudulent manipulation of TRX’s secondary market;
extensive wash trading;
sales into a market whose apparent activity had allegedly been inflated; and
payments to celebrities who promoted TRX or BTT without disclosing their compensation.

These were allegations in a civil complaint, not criminal convictions or adjudicated factual findings against Sun. SEC, March 22, 2023 complaint announcement

IV. Alleged wash-trading structure

The SEC alleged that from approximately April 2018 through February 2019, Sun directed employees to conduct more than 600,000 TRX wash trades between two accounts he controlled.

The alleged daily volume ranged from approximately 4.5 million to 7.4 million TRX.

According to the SEC, the alleged transactions:

involved simultaneous or nearly simultaneous buying and selling;
produced no genuine change in beneficial ownership;
created a misleading appearance of active trading;
increased apparent market demand; and
accompanied sales through which Sun allegedly generated approximately $31 million.

These allegations were ultimately not adjudicated against Sun.

V. Undisclosed promotional payments

The SEC also alleged that Sun and affiliated entities paid celebrities to promote TRX and BTT without adequate disclosure.

Named promoters included:

Lindsay Lohan;
Jake Paul;
Soulja Boy;
Austin Mahone;
Kendra Lust;
Lil Yachty;
Ne-Yo; and
Akon.

Most of the named celebrity defendants settled separately without admitting or denying the SEC’s findings. Those settlements did not establish Sun’s liability.

VI. Litigation posture before the Trump investments

Before the 2024 election, the SEC litigation presented Sun with potential exposure involving:

injunctive relief;
civil penalties;
disgorgement;
market-conduct restrictions;
findings concerning wash trading;
restrictions on token promotion;
reputational damage;
exchange-listing consequences; and
possible collateral consequences for affiliated ventures.

The value of obtaining a stay, dismissal, or restricted settlement was therefore potentially substantial even without a direct monetary payment from the government.

VII. Initial World Liberty Financial purchase

In November 2024, after Donald Trump won the presidential election but before his inauguration, Sun publicly announced a purchase of approximately $30 million in World Liberty Financial tokens.

The transaction reportedly made Sun one of the venture’s largest outside purchasers at a time when its initial token offering had experienced limited demand.

World Liberty Financial subsequently announced or acknowledged that Sun had become an adviser.

Investigators should verify:

the purchasing wallet;
the precise token amount;
the purchase price;
the source of funds;
any side agreement;
advisory compensation;
governance rights;
transfer restrictions;
refund rights; and
communications preceding the purchase.

VIII. Additional January 2025 investment

Immediately before President Trump’s January 20, 2025 inauguration, Sun announced an additional purchase of approximately $45 million in World Liberty Financial tokens.

This reportedly brought his aggregate commitment to approximately $75 million.

